{
  "raw": {
    "https://careers.widener.edu/en-us/job/493440/director-center-for-teaching-and-learning": {
      "title": "Director, Center for Teaching and Learning",
      "description": "Director, Center for Teaching and Learning Apply now Job no: 493440 Work type: Full-time Location: Chester Categories: Administrators Widener University is currently seeking an innovative and strategic Director for its Center for Teaching and Learning. Reporting to the Vice Provost for Academic Affairs, the Director will oversee the University’s efforts to promote teaching excellence and provide opportunities for continuous improvement and professional development. This role will identify, develop, and oversee a rich and diverse set of programming including: new faculty orientation, in-house training, guest speakers, and external events. The Director will promote teaching excellence and best practices by regularly meeting with faculty as well as maintaining a space for collaboration, interdepartmental projects, and idea sharing. The Director will assist with institutional and program assessment activities and help faculty with assessment processes for continuous improvement of student learning. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES (including, but not limited to) : Essential Duties: Faculty Development and Support Design, implement, and oversee comprehensive faculty development initiatives, including new faculty orientation and faculty mentoring programs. Identify, develop, and coordinate internal and external professional development opportunities that support excellence in teaching, learning, scholarship, and leadership. Collaborate with faculty, librarians, instructional designers, instructional technologists, and other subject matter experts to deliver innovative and impactful professional development programming. Coordinate and manage faculty and staff micro-credentialing initiatives that promote continuous learning and professional growth. Teaching Excellence and Innovation Foster a culture of continuous improvement, innovation, and excellence in teaching and learning across the University. Promote evidence-based instructional practices and emerging pedagogical strategies that enhance student engagement and success. Support faculty in the exploration and adoption of effective teaching methodologies, educational technologies, and learner-centered approaches. Community Building and Collaboration Create and facilitate opportunities for faculty to collaborate, share best practices, exchange ideas, and engage in interdisciplinary dialogue. Build and sustain a vibrant community of practice that encourages professional engagement, innovation, and collegial support. Maintain a visible and active presence across academic units and campus locations to strengthen relationships and promote collaboration among faculty and staff. Assessment and Institutional Effectiveness Promote and support institutional, co-curricular, program, and course assessment initiatives that advance student learning and institutional effectiveness. Collaborate with academic and administrative stakeholders to develop and sustain assessment practices that foster continuous improvement and informed decision-making. Assist faculty and departments in using assessment data to evaluate outcomes, improve programs, and enhance educational quality. Strategic Leadership and Outreach Provide leadership for Center for Teaching and Learning initiatives that align with the University's mission, strategic priorities, and accreditation expectations. Develop outreach strategies that increase participation in Center programs and services while strengthening engagement across the campus community. Serve as a resource and advocate for teaching excellence, faculty success, and lifelong professional learning throughout the University. Expected Outcomes Advance excellence in teaching and learning through innovative faculty development programs. Strengthen faculty engagement, collaboration, and professional growth. Enhance assessment practices that support student success and continuous institutional improvement. Promote an inclusive, collaborative, and learner-centered academic culture. Secondary Responsibilities: Perform other job-related duties as assigned or directed to support departmental needs and align with the mission, values and goals of the Provost’s Office and Widener University. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: (education/training and experience required ) Master’s degree from an accredited institution. Three (3+) years of experience teaching at the collegiate level. Experience in the assessment of student learning outcomes at the course, program, and institutional levels. Experience with artificial intelligence (AI) tools and technologies in a higher education setting. Knowledge of evidence-based teaching practices, instructional innovation, and emerging trends in higher education pedagogy. Ability to collaborate effectively with faculty, staff, and administrators across multiple departments and disciplines. Demonstrated commitment to continuous professional growth and development. All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Widener University is committed to fostering an inclusive community in which faculty, staff, and students from a variety of backgrounds, cultures, and personal experiences are welcomed and can thrive. We are an equal opportunity employer and are committed to providing equal educational and employment opportunities for all persons without regard to age, color, national origin, race, religion, disability, veteran status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, or status as a protected veteran. Some positions may be eligible for a hybrid or remote work arrangement that may include a partially or fully remote work location, consistent with Widener’s Flexible Work policy. Widener University, an independent, metropolitan, doctoral-intensive university, connects curricula to social issues through civic engagement. Dynamic teaching, active scholarship, personal attention and experiential learning are key components of the Widener Experience. Located in Chester, PA, Widener's main campus is nestled between Philadelphia, PA and Wilmington, DE, with Law Schools located in both Harrisburg and Wilmington. For more information about the university, please visit our website at www.widener.edu. EOE M/F/V/D Advertised: 13 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.widener.edu/en-us/job/493447/coordinating-support-specialist": {
      "title": "Coordinating Support Specialist",
      "description": "Coordinating Support Specialist Apply now Job no: 493447 Work type: Full-time (hourly) Location: Chester Categories: Staff he Coordinating Support Specialist in the College of Health and Human Services reports to the Director of Institute Graduate Clinical Psychology in the College and provides support to interprofessional spaces, curriculum, events, and clinics within the college and across campus. The College, which has a focus on interprofessional education, service, and research, houses programs in clinical psychology, education, human sexuality, occupational therapy, physical therapy, physician assistant, speech-language pathology and social work. As Coordinating Support Specialist for the interprofessional endeavors of the college, decisions will affect operations and functions of various units within the college and require superior interpersonal and collaborative skills. Duties are moderately complex & varied, and analytic ability is required. The position requires time management, multi-tasking, and future planning skills as well as handling confidential information with integrity. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES (including, but not limited to) : Essential Duties: Establish a welcoming, inclusive environment for all constituents of the Institute and College Administrative Team through effective communication and interactions with persons of diverse backgrounds and abilities. Serve a first point of contact for communications with the Institute. Handles communications in a professional manner, responds to inquiries, screens and directs callers appropriately. Communicate, collect and organize all information that contributes to smooth operation of the Institute included by not limited to cohort schedules, Institute, College, and University calendars, course syllabi, faculty schedules, course evaluations, and student registration. Participate in the collection and synthesis of data needed for accreditation reports. Work with the Program Director and College Administrative Team to manage budget, faculty adjunct and overload contracts. Work with Program Director for the ordering of office & instructional supplies, book orders, special orders, and processing of expense reports. Organize new student orientation and events, graduation banquet, and other special events significant to the Institute and across the College Support the Program Director in the management of scholarship applications and awards. Manage graduation processing and licensure verification for students within the Institute Monitor Graduate Assistants and Student Workers within the Institute. Oversee physical plant and housekeeping of relevant spaces. Maintain calendar, appointments and meetings for the Institute Program Director. Secondary Responsibilities: Support all members of the College Administrative Team in tasks as needed. Work collaboratively with the Team to contribute to a positive work environment. Serve on specific College Administrative Teams as indicated. Assist in the coordination of College special events as needed. Perform other job-related duties as assigned or directed to support departmental needs and align with the mission, values and goals of the Institute Graduate Clinical Psychology and Widener University. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS (education/training and experience required) : Required: High school diploma or general education degree. One (1+) year of administrative assistant experience in a service-oriented environment or any combination of experience, training or education, which provides the necessary skills, ability and knowledge. Prior experience in use of personal computers, applicable software and the internet; intermediate to advanced proficiency in Microsoft Office. Ability to perform basic arithmetic functions, bookkeeping, basic accounting, and basic statistics including programming of Excel spreadsheets Competence with data entry and ability to learn university systems, Datatel, Colleague, Slate, Office365, Teams, and OneDrive. Ability to navigate web-based programs including, but not limited to, learning management system (Canvas) and teleconference software (Zoom). Superior interpersonal skills and oral and written communication skills. Preferred: Three (3+) years of administrative support experience in a service-oriented environment. PHYSICAL REQUIREMENTS AND/OR UNUSUAL HOURS: Ability to sit and work at computer for long periods. Ability to conduct business on phone and answer in a timely manner. Ability to move between offices, floors and buildings on campus. Some evenings and weekends may be required for events. All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Widener University is committed to fostering an inclusive community in which faculty, staff, and students from a variety of backgrounds, cultures, and personal experiences are welcomed and can thrive. We are an equal opportunity employer and are committed to providing equal educational and employment opportunities for all persons without regard to age, color, national origin, race, religion, disability, veteran status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, or status as a protected veteran. Some positions may be eligible for a hybrid or remote work arrangement that may include a partially or fully remote work location, consistent with Widener’s Flexible Work policy. Widener University, an independent, metropolitan, doctoral-intensive university, connects curricula to social issues through civic engagement. Dynamic teaching, active scholarship, personal attention and experiential learning are key components of the Widener Experience. Located in Chester, PA, Widener's main campus is nestled between Philadelphia, PA and Wilmington, DE, with Law Schools located in both Harrisburg and Wilmington. For more information about the university, please visit our website at www.widener.edu. EOE M/F/V/D Advertised: 13 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.widener.edu/en-us/job/493446/lecturer-of-business": {
      "title": "Lecturer of Business",
      "description": "Lecturer of Business Apply now Job no: 493446 Work type: Faculty (Non-tenure track) Location: Chester Categories: Faculty Widener University is seeking a non-tenure-track Lecturer of Business in the School of Business Administration. Reporting to the Dean of the School of Business Administration, this is a 10-month, full-time, non-tenure-track position. This position is designed to provide broad instructional capacity across the business core, while strengthening academic advising, assessment, and other academic functions of the school. The position does not require research; however, it does involve serving on departmental and university committees and other service to the profession and Widener students. The successful candidate will teach business core courses based on academic qualifications and school needs and will provide instructional flexibility across multiple undergraduate business programs. Courses will be delivered in a variety of modalities, primarily in person on the Chester Campus, but may also include online and hybrid formats. The educator will employ innovative teaching practices, demonstrate a deep commitment to student success, and adapt to varied learning needs. The faculty member’s expertise and pedagogical excellence will play a vital role in advancing the department’s mission and vision, fostering academic growth, and preparing students for professional achievement in the field of business. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES (including, but not limited to) : Essential Duties: Teaching, Instruction, and Assessment Teach undergraduate courses in the general business core, aligned with the candidate’s expertise. Teach a 3/4 course load, primarily in person, with opportunities for online or hybrid instruction. Foster student success through engaging, practical, and career-focused instruction. Contribute to course- and program-level assessment of learning activities. Service and Leadership Actively participate in departmental and university committees. Provide academic advising and career guidance to students. Contribute to departmental administrative responsibilities and support professional initiatives. Engage in service activities that benefit the University, the profession, and the broader community. Secondary Responsibilities: Perform other job-related duties as assigned or directed to support departmental needs and align with the mission of the School of Business and Accounting department. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS (education/training and experience required) : Required: Master’s degree in a business field from an AACSB-accredited business program by the date of appointment. Degree must be from an accredited University or College. ABD and Terminal Degree candidates will be eligible for consideration. College or university-level experience teaching undergraduate business courses. Strong commitment to student achievement, with a focus on guiding learners in developing a portfolio of marketable skills and competencies that enhance career readiness. Dedication to excellence in teaching, demonstrated through the use of innovative, engaging, and student-centered pedagogical approaches. Preferred: Broad knowledge of business concepts and professional experience . Non-Tenure Track This is a non-tenure-track position, meaning the lecturer, based on qualifications, may be offered a one-year letter of appointment with the potential for ongoing three-year appointments. All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Widener University is committed to fostering an inclusive community in which faculty, staff, and students from a variety of backgrounds, cultures, and personal experiences are welcomed and can thrive. We are an equal opportunity employer and are committed to providing equal educational and employment opportunities for all persons without regard to age, color, national origin, race, religion, disability, veteran status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, or status as a protected veteran. Some positions may be eligible for a hybrid or remote work arrangement that may include a partially or fully remote work location, consistent with Widener’s Flexible Work policy. Widener University, an independent, metropolitan, doctoral-intensive university, connects curricula to social issues through civic engagement. Dynamic teaching, active scholarship, personal attention, and experiential learning are key components of the Widener Experience. Located in Chester, PA, Widener's main campus is nestled between Philadelphia, PA, and Wilmington, DE, with Law Schools located in both Harrisburg and Wilmington. For more information about the university, please visit our website at www.widener.edu. EOE M/F/V/D Advertised: 07 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.widener.edu/en-us/job/493445/director-center-for-education": {
      "title": "Director, Center for Education",
      "description": "Director, Center for Education Apply now Job no: 493445 Work type: Faculty (Tenure track) Location: Chester Categories: Faculty Widener University is seeking a Director of the Center for Education in a 12-month appointment beginning in Fall 2027. The Director of the Center for Education (CED) holds a dual academic leadership role, including rank and title as a tenure-track faculty member. This role is responsible for the overall management and administration of the programs and activities of the Center. The Director serves as the strategic, academic, and operational leader for the Center, focusing on curriculum oversight, regulatory compliance, programmatic fiscal management, personnel supervision, and ensuring alignment with the University’s mission and strategic plan. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES (including, but not limited to) : Essential Duties: Provide primary oversight and direction of the Center’s academic, research, and clinical programs. Provide leadership for the selection, evaluation, and supervision of faculty and staff, including effective and timely management of personnel challenges and providing faculty mentoring. In consultation with the College Dean and Associate Dean of Human Services, develop and manage the Center’s budget. Develop and implement a program strategic plan that is congruent with Widener University’s strategy and plans, and that supports ongoing strategic partnerships. Provide supervision, direction, and appropriate evaluation, where appropriate, of individuals who are direct reports to the Center Director. Cultivate and expand relationships with local educational agencies (LEAs) to secure field and student teaching placements, including recruitment, orientation, and ongoing support for cooperating teachers and student teaching supervisors. Oversee the coordination of student teaching and field placements each semester, including scheduling, confirmation, and supervision assignments, ensuring compliance with Pennsylvania Department of Education (PDE) requirements. Serve as the Center’s Certifying Officer and primary liaison for PDE. Direct ongoing review and evaluation activities to maintain compliance with PDE standards and alignment with relevant guidelines/standards associated with teacher preparation programs (e.g. Title II reporting). Lead the faculty in curriculum development, implementation, review, and continuous improvement. Coordinate faculty professional development in collaboration with faculty. Manage faculty course load and adjunct faculty needs. Direct student selection process and participate in student recruitment in collaboration with Undergraduate Admissions. Create and implement strategies in support of student recruitment, retention and success. Develop and maintain the program website, Student Handbooks, and policies and procedures for directing the Center’s programs. Manage review of student performance, retention and probationary processes, including advising and advocating for students. Oversee program evaluation and student learning outcome assessment processes for the Center’s programs. Develop and teach courses in the Center’s programs. Demonstrate competency in knowledge and skills in teaching as evidenced by supervisor and student evaluations. Engage in scholarship activities and maintain excellence in professional development. Participation in university shared governance responsibilities, including ex officio role on committees as identified in unit or college bylaws. Maintain regular office hours. Secondary Responsibilities Participate, where appropriate, in team and/or interdisciplinary teaching and scholarship. Develop collaborative partnerships within and outside Widener University, including maintaining clinical and community partnerships, alumni, and development relations. Assist with grant writing and external funding opportunities. Perform other job-related duties as assigned or directed to support departmental needs and align with the mission, values and goals of the Center for Education and Widener University. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS (education/training and experience required) : Required: An academic doctoral degree (PhD, EdD, DSc, or equivalent) in a field relevant to Education. Degree must be from an accredited University or College. If applicable based on discipline or field, current licensure/eligibility for licensure in the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania. Current experience with PDE standards and external compliance requirements for teacher preparation programs. Five (5+) years’ experience working in a relevant field with evidence of participating in practice and/or administrative responsibilities. Previous administrative experience in the following: personnel management; evaluation; program budgeting; and program planning and implementation. Three (3+) years’ experience in higher education, with the potential to be appointed to the faculty at the relevant rank and title. A record of teaching effectiveness and scholarly inquiry in an area of practice, research and/or administration. Excellent interpersonal skills, and written and oral communication abilities. Demonstrated knowledge of education, certification, and scope of practice in teaching and teacher preparation. Strong knowledge of health and human services delivery system structures. Demonstrated commitment to belonging, inclusion, and success in working with diverse populations. Preferred: Previous experience in Education Leadership, such as Program Director, Associate Program Director, Academic Director, or Internship Director. Experience in accreditation, curricular evaluation and design, and assessment. Experience in programmatic fiscal and personnel management. An active member of relevant professional associations. A record of demonstrated advocacy and leadership in education and/or practice at the local, state, and/or national level. PHYSICAL REQUIREMENTS AND/OR UNUSUAL HOURS: Usual office environment; some walk-in traffic; high level of interaction with others in person, electronically, or via telephone. Some evening and weekend hours may be required as determined by student enrollment needs. Ability to work at a computer for extended periods of time. Ability to carry files and work items weighing up to 20 lbs. Ability to travel within a 50-mile radius. Ability to be mobile between workstation and various campus buildings. All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Widener University is committed to fostering an inclusive community in which faculty, staff, and students from a variety of backgrounds, cultures, and personal experiences are welcomed and can thrive. We are an equal opportunity employer and are committed to providing equal educational and employment opportunities for all persons without regard to age, color, national origin, race, religion, disability, veteran status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, or status as a protected veteran. Some positions may be eligible for a hybrid or remote work arrangement that may include a partially or fully remote work location, consistent with Widener’s Flexible Work policy. Widener University, an independent, metropolitan, doctoral-intensive university, connects curricula to social issues through civic engagement. Dynamic teaching, active scholarship, personal attention and experiential learning are key components of the Widener Experience. Located in Chester, PA, Widener's main campus is nestled between Philadelphia, PA and Wilmington, DE, with Law Schools located in both Harrisburg and Wilmington. For more information about the university, please visit our website at www.widener.edu. EOE M/F/V/D Advertised: 06 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.widener.edu/en-us/job/493444/library-collections-assistant": {
      "title": "Library Collections Assistant",
      "description": "Library Collections Assistant Apply now Job no: 493444 Work type: Full-time (hourly) Location: Chester Categories: Staff Widener University Wolfgram Memorial Library is seeking a Library Collections Assistant to oversee the full lifecycle of continuing resources, including journals, magazines, etc. in both print and digital formats. This role involves processing incoming issues, maintaining accurate subscription records, and claiming missing materials. The position requires troubleshooting access issues, coordinating with vendors, and supporting operational efficiency. Reporting to the Head of Collections & Resource Management, the Library Collections Assistant is responsible for daily operation of the Serials Department. Assists in formulating departmental policies and procedures. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES (including, but not limited to): Essential Duties: Responsible for operations of serials Checkin app of the FOLIO library system. Works closely with Head of Collections and Resource Management. Uses FOLIO serials app for check-in and claiming of print serial issues. Executes orders for new subscriptions, renewals and cancellations. Acts as Serials Department liaison with university Accounts Payable office and serial vendors. Maintains financial data related to serials subscriptions, and all professional bindery operations. Maintains all serials department financial statistics. Works with Head of Collections and Resource Management to identify new titles and title changes in need of cataloging. Manages the record keeping for “problem titles”. Claims missing issues on a timely basis, using automated claiming modules. Sorts and distributes mail on a daily basis. Secondary Responsibilities: Assists in the Resource Sharing & Interlibrary Loan (ILL) Department, one to two hours per day as needed. Assists in providing Virtual Assistance Services. Assists in staffing the Library Information Desk as needed. Perform other job-related duties as assigned or directed to support departmental needs and align with the mission, values and goals of the Wolfgram Memorial Library and Widener University. Participate in periodic staff meetings and in-service trainings. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS (education/training and experience required): Required: Two (2+) years’ experience working in automated library environment. Excellent interpersonal skills and attention to detail. Ability and willingness to learn new technology tools including generative AI. Preferred: Post-secondary education and/or equivalent experience and learning skills. Copy-cataloging skills Familiarity with FOLIO. Familiarity with OCLC MARC records. Familiarity with ILLiad and/or EZBorrow. Supervisory experience and ability to meet challenges of managing a busy department. PHYSICAL REQUIREMENTS AND/OR UNUSUAL HOURS: Ability to lift heavy (40 lb.) bound and unbound volumes. All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Widener University is committed to fostering an inclusive community in which faculty, staff, and students from a variety of backgrounds, cultures, and personal experiences are welcomed and can thrive. We are an equal opportunity employer and are committed to providing equal educational and employment opportunities for all persons without regard to age, color, national origin, race, religion, disability, veteran status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, or status as a protected veteran. Some positions may be eligible for a hybrid or remote work arrangement that may include a partially or fully remote work location, consistent with Widener’s Flexible Work policy. Widener University, an independent, metropolitan, doctoral-intensive university, connects curricula to social issues through civic engagement. Dynamic teaching, active scholarship, personal attention and experiential learning are key components of the Widener Experience. Located in Chester, PA, Widener's main campus is nestled between Philadelphia, PA and Wilmington, DE, with Law Schools located in both Harrisburg and Wilmington. For more information about the university, please visit our website at www.widener.edu. EOE M/F/V/D Advertised: 02 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.widener.edu/en-us/job/493442/assistant-professor-of-practice-clinical-sexology": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Practice, Clinical Sexology",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Practice, Clinical Sexology Apply now Job no: 493442 Work type: Faculty (Non-tenure track) Location: Chester Categories: Faculty Widener University is currently seeking a non-tenure track Assistant Professor of Practice in Clinical Sexology who would work in the Center for Human Sexuality Studies and would report to the Director of the Center for Human Sexuality Studies and the Dean of the College of Health and Human Services. This is a 10- month position with teaching and service duties and non-tenure track status. Center for Human Sexuality Studies (CHSS): CHSS is housed within the College of Health and Human Services, which also includes programs in Clinical Psychology, Physical Therapy, Social Work, Education, Occupational Therapy, Physician Assistant Education and Speech and Language Pathology. Offering Masters and Doctoral degrees, and a variety of professional development and continuing education programs, CHSS is a unique program nationally, providing professional and scholarly education in human sexuality with concentrations in sexuality education, sex therapy, public policy, and non-profit management. The program draws students from across the country and internationally. To make our programs available for working adults, we offer courses that usually meet on weekends and weeknights in online formats. CHSS has a strong commitment to social justice and the intersections of multiple identities and perspectives. We are seeking a candidate who reflects these core values in their work. For more information see widener.edu/humansexuality. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES (including, but not limited to) : Essential Duties: 3/3 load, teaching three graduate courses each Fall and Spring semester. Committee and other service to the institution. Doctoral dissertation advisement. Secondary Responsibilities: Graduate student advising. Professional development (publishing scholarly work, presenting original research or practice recommendations, etc.). Overload teaching may be available. Perform other job-related duties as assigned or directed to support departmental needs and align with the mission, values and goals of the Center for Human Sexuality Studies and Widener University. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS (education/training and experience required) : Required: Doctoral degree in human sexuality or a related field. We understand that the field of human sexuality draws from a variety of disciplines. As such, we invite applicants to make a solid case in their cover letters as to how their training prepares them to join CHSS. Valid and current mental health license. AASECT Certified Sex Therapist with familiarity with ASSECT certification procedures and requirements. AASECT approved supervisor. Experience teaching at undergraduate or graduate level. Ability to teach in online formats. Highest degree from an accredited university or college Preferred: Experience teaching at the graduate level. Experience mentoring doctoral students writing dissertations. A teacher-scholar with a developing or established. research/publication program in human sexuality. Our faculty are experts in the areas of academic research, clinical training, and educational methods and we seek a new faculty member who will strengthen our work in one or more of these areas. PHYSICAL REQUIREMENTS AND/OR UNUSUAL HOURS: We offer online courses that meet on weekends and weeknights. To apply: Applications will be considered until the job is filled but for full consideration, applications should be completed by November 15, 2026. The application requires a cover letter, and a current CV. Incomplete applications will not be considered. Only those selected to interview will be contacted. Additional information may be requested after an initial review of applications. All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Widener University is committed to fostering an inclusive community in which faculty, staff, and students from a variety of backgrounds, cultures, and personal experiences are welcomed and can thrive. We are an equal opportunity employer and are committed to providing equal educational and employment opportunities for all persons without regard to age, color, national origin, race, religion, disability, veteran status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, or status as a protected veteran. This position is eligible for a fully remote work location, consistent with Widener’s Flexible Work policy. U.S work authorization is required. Widener University, an independent, metropolitan, doctoral-intensive university, connects curricula to social issues through civic engagement. Dynamic teaching, active scholarship, personal attention and experiential learning are key components of the Widener Experience. Located in Chester, PA, Widener's main campus is nestled between Philadelphia, PA and Wilmington, DE, with Law Schools located in both Harrisburg and Wilmington. For more information about the university, please visit our website at www.widener.edu. EOE M/F/V/D Advertised: 24 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.widener.edu/en-us/job/493443/assistant-professor-of-computer-science": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Computer Science",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Computer Science Apply now Job no: 493443 Work type: Faculty (Tenure track) Location: Chester Categories: Faculty Reporting to the Dean of the College of Arts & Sciences, the Assistant Professor of Computer Science is a full-time, tenure-track position beginning in Fall 2027. The Department of Computer Science at Widener University seeks candidates with strong teaching experience who are passionate about student success and committed to creating an environment of inclusive excellence where our students can thrive and achieve success in their chosen fields. We welcome candidates who are enthusiastic about teaching, research, mentoring undergraduate students, contributing to a collaborative academic community, and helping shape the future growth of our programs. The successful candidate is expected to teach three or four courses per semester, actively engage in research in their field of interest, serve on university, college, and/or department committees, and advise undergraduate students. The Department of Computer Science at Widener University is a small, collegial, and student-focused department that provides a supportive environment for both faculty and students. The department offers undergraduate programs in Computer Science, Computer Information Systems, the interdisciplinary Digital Media Informatics, and our new Bachelor of Science program in Artificial Intelligence. Those candidates experienced in, or who would enjoy, working with a diverse range of faculty, staff and students, and those who can contribute to the climate of inclusivity are encouraged to apply. Applicants must submit a cover letter, curriculum vita, statement of teaching philosophy, statement of research, and evidence teaching effectiveness. Additionally, three confidential letters of reference and any additional related materials must be sent to artsandsciences@widener.edu. Applications will be reviewed starting 12/11/2026. Only those selected for an interview will be contacted. For more information about Widener University and the Department of Computer Science, see www.widener.edu . DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES (including, but not limited to) : Essential duties: Teaching Teach lecture classes for first- and second-year Computer Science majors and non-majors. Teach upper-level electives for junior and senior Computer Science majors and non-majors. Develop course materials and assessments that foster critical thinking and content mastery. Evaluate student performance and provide constructive feedback. Maintain regular weekly office hours to provide academic support and guidance to students enrolled in assigned courses. Research To be active in research in their field of interest To involve students in research Mentorship Advise and mentor undergraduate students. Service Attend division, College, and University faculty meetings and contribute to department initiatives. Secondary Responsibilities: Perform other job-related duties as assigned and participation in the intellectual life of the Department, the College of Arts & Sciences, and the University. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS (education/training and experience required) : Required: Ph.D. in Computer Science or closely related field. Strong communication and interpersonal skills. Experience and commitment to excellence in teaching at the college level. Commitment to equity, inclusion and belonging in teaching and service. Active in research in their field of interest Highest degree from an accredited university or college PHYSICAL REQUIREMENTS AND/OR UNUSUAL HOURS: Must be available for in-person instruction. Must be able to sit for extended periods while working at a computer or grading papers, and the ability lecture in a classroom. Must have the ability to speak clearly and audibly to deliver lectures, facilitate classroom discussions and interact with students and colleagues. Must have the ability to move around campus to attend meetings and teach in different classrooms. Minimal lifting may be involved for tasks such as carrying materials to class or moving books. All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Widener University is committed to fostering an inclusive community in which faculty, staff, and students from a variety of backgrounds, cultures, and personal experiences are welcomed and can thrive. We are an equal opportunity employer and are committed to providing equal educational and employment opportunities for all persons without regard to age, color, national origin, race, religion, disability, veteran status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, or status as a protected veteran. U.S. work authorization is required. Widener University, an independent, metropolitan, doctoral-intensive university, connects curricula to social issues through civic engagement. Dynamic teaching, active scholarship, personal attention and experiential learning are key components of the Widener Experience. Located in Chester, PA, Widener's main campus is nestled between Philadelphia, PA and Wilmington, DE, with Law Schools located in both Harrisburg and Wilmington. For more information about the university, please visit our website at www.widener.edu. EOE M/F/V/D Advertised: 24 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.widener.edu/en-us/job/493441/faculty-commonwealth-law-school": {
      "title": "Faculty- Commonwealth Law School",
      "description": "Faculty- Commonwealth Law School Apply now Job no: 493441 Work type: Faculty (Tenure track) Location: Harrisburg Categories: Faculty Widener Law Commonwealth seeks an entry-level or pre-tenure lateral faculty member to fill a tenure track position. We have specific needs in our year-long Property and Legal Methods courses. As to the rest of the teaching package, we have flexibility, but we have particular needs in the areas of Administrative Law, Contracts, Commercial Law, Environmental Law, and Intellectual Property. Established in 1989, Widener Law Commonwealth is an independently accredited law school within Widener University. Located in Harrisburg, PA, the law school’s location in the capital of Pennsylvania provides impactful experiences for both our faculty and students. WLC is a dynamic community of teachers and scholars. We pride ourselves on our dedication to our students, our engagement with teaching, and our scholarly impact. Many of our scholars are actively engaged in law reform efforts at both the state and federal level. The law school is committed to fostering an environment in which faculty, staff, and students from a variety of backgrounds, cultures, and personal experiences are welcomed and can thrive. Faculty and staff are active participants in our work to enhance diversity, equity, inclusion, and belonging. We welcome applications from members of historically underrepresented groups. We are looking for candidates with strong academic backgrounds, commitment to excellence in teaching, and demonstrated potential to be productive scholars. Candidates must possess at least a JD degree or its equivalent. Advertised: 21 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.widener.edu/en-us/job/493437/speechlanguage-pathology-faculty": {
      "title": "Speech-Language Pathology Faculty",
      "description": "Speech-Language Pathology Faculty Apply now Job no: 493437 Work type: Faculty (Tenure track) Location: Chester Categories: Faculty Widener University is seeking a 10‑month, tenure‑track faculty position within the Institute for Speech-Language Pathology. The successful candidate will join a dynamic, interprofessional academic community and contribute to the growth of a forward‑thinking program committed to excellence in teaching, clinical education, research, and service. Candidates must hold an academic or research doctorate in Speech-Language Pathology (SLP), Communication Sciences and Disorders, or a related discipline; possess ASHA Certification in Speech-Language Pathology (CCC‑SLP); be eligible for Pennsylvania SLP licensure; and demonstrate a minimum of three years of clinical, teaching, and research experience in higher education. Preference will be given to applicants with expertise in pediatric and/or school‑aged communication disorders, bilingual speech and language development, or bilingual assessment and intervention practices with pediatric and/or school‑aged populations. The Institute for Speech-Language Pathology is part of Widener’s College of Health & Human Services, a collaborative academic environment that emphasizes interprofessional education, community engagement, and impactful research. The college includes programs in physical therapy, occupational therapy, physician assistant studies, clinical psychology, education, human sexuality, and social work. Our students bring a wide range of identities, perspectives, and lived experiences, and we seek faculty who are committed to fostering an inclusive and supportive learning environment. The Speech-Language Pathology Faculty for the Institute for Speech-Language Pathology in the College of Health & Human Services is a full‑time, 10‑month, tenure‑track faculty position involved in a combination of teaching, research/scholarly activity, clinical supervision, and service to the institution and profession. This academic-year role generally focuses on educating graduate students, maintaining an active research agenda, and ensuring compliance with ASHA accreditation standards. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES (including, but not limited to) : Essential Duties: Teaching Develop and teach courses in the SLP Program as negotiated with the Director. Ensure courses and syllabi are current and available to all students. Demonstrate competency in teaching as evidenced by supervisor, peer, and student evaluations. Demonstration of expertise in areas of teaching responsibility and content delivery method. Participate in team and/or interdisciplinary teaching. Participate in curriculum development. Use faculty development opportunities and teacher effectiveness tools to improve teaching. Supervise students on special projects (e.g. interprofessional, research projects). Maintain regular office hours, guide or support students who reach out for assistance, and identify students who are struggling and need extra attention or support to be successful through use of email, phone or other appropriate means of communication. Clinical Education Supervision of student clinical education in the SLP Clinic and affiliated interprofessional pro bono Chester Community Clinic. Scholarship/Practice Conduct and disseminate applied research. Engage in scholarly/clinical work related to area of interest on a regular basis and as required by expectations of the Program. Maintain currency of clinical and professional knowledge through regular continuing education. Service Attend all university academic events as required in the faculty handbook. Attend all University, College, and Program faculty meetings. Participate in student recruitment and admission activities. Engage with students via co-curricular activities. Serve as an academic advisor and/or mentor for students and/or student organizations. Assist in the accreditation process including preparation of documents for accreditation applications, annual reviews, and site visits. Demonstrate the ability to support the mission of Widener University as evidenced by teaching and clinical assignments, advising, and mentoring of students. Participate in professional activities and service to the University, College, community, and the profession. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS (education/training and experience required) : Required: An academic/research doctorate (PhD, EdD) in Speech-Language Pathology, Communication Sciences and Disorders, or a related field from an accredited college or university. Certification in Speech-Language Pathology by the American Speech-Language-Hearing Association (CCC-SLP). Licensure in the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania, or eligibility to apply, in Speech-Language Pathology. Minimum 5 years clinical experience in speech-language pathology. Minimum 3 years clinical, teaching, and research experience in an institution of higher education. In-depth knowledge of at least one area of SLP practice and scientific knowledge base. Knowledge of education and health care system structures relative to SLP. Excellent interpersonal skills and written and oral communication abilities. Strong organizational skills. Knowledge of content delivery methods including web-supported classes (e.g., Canvas) and distance learning. Experience networking with various academic disciplines to promote interprofessional education and practice among faculty and students. Preferred: 5 + years clinical, teaching, and research experience in an institution of higher education. 7 + years clinical experience in speech-language pathology. Experience with bilingual or multilingual clinical service delivery. Active participation in local, regional, national, or international SLP organizations. PHYSICAL REQUIREMENTS AND/OR UNUSUAL HOURS: Ability to sit, stand, and move throughout classrooms and campus spaces for extended periods. Capacity to lift and transport instructional materials or supplies weighing up to 20 lbs. Availability to support students by phone, email, or other communication channels during evenings and weekends as needed. All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Widener University is committed to fostering an inclusive community in which faculty, staff, and students from a variety of backgrounds, cultures, and personal experiences are welcomed and can thrive. We are an equal opportunity employer and are committed to providing equal educational and employment opportunities for all persons without regard to age, color, national origin, race, religion, disability, veteran status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, or status as a protected veteran. Widener University, an independent, metropolitan, doctoral-intensive university, connects curricula to social issues through civic engagement. Dynamic teaching, active scholarship, personal attention and experiential learning are key components of the Widener Experience. Located in Chester, PA, Widener's main campus is nestled between Philadelphia, PA and Wilmington, DE, with Law Schools located in both Harrisburg and Wilmington. For more information about the university, please visit our website at www.widener.edu. EOE M/F/V/D Advertised: 15 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.widener.edu/en-us/job/493438/assistant-professor-of-criminal-justice": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Criminal Justice",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Criminal Justice Apply now Job no: 493438 Work type: Faculty (Tenure track) Location: Chester Categories: Faculty The Criminal Justice department at Widener University seeks applicants for a Tenure Track Assistant Professor of Criminal Justice to begin August 2027. This position will have a teaching load of 4/3 and involves teaching at the undergraduate and graduate level. The candidate is also expected to serve as an academic advisor to undergraduate criminal justice majors, assist with supervising interns, and participate in academic citizenship. The area of specialization is open, but versatility across research methods, criminal justice ethics, and criminological theory would be preferred. The Criminal Justice Department is housed in the Social Science Division of the College of Arts & Sciences and offers a BA and MA in Criminal Justice. The department has 5 full-time faculty members and is the second largest major in the division. Our program focuses on theory and application to prepare students for a variety of pathways, including law school, graduate school, and working in all parts of the criminal justice system. The department values experiential learning for our students and requires an internship prior to graduation. Those candidates experienced in, or who would enjoy, working with a diverse range of faculty, staff and students, and those who can contribute to the climate of inclusivity are encouraged to apply. Applicants must submit a cover letter, curriculum vita, statement of teaching philosophy, and evidence of teaching effectiveness. Additionally, three confidential letters of reference must be sent to artsandsciences@widener.edu. Applications will no longer be accepted after October 31, 2026. Applications will be reviewed starting November 1 st . Only those selected for an interview will be contacted. For more information about Widener University and the Department of Criminal Justice, see www.widener.edu . DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES (including, but not limited to) : Essential duties: Teaching 4/3 teaching load Develop course materials and assessments that foster critical thinking and content mastery. Evaluate student performance and provide constructive feedback. Maintain regular weekly office hours to provide academic support and guidance to students enrolled in assigned courses. Mentorship Advise and mentor undergraduate students. Research Establish and maintain a successful research program in the candidate’s field or discipline Engage and mentor undergraduate students in their research program Service Attend division, College, and University faculty meetings and contribute to department initiatives. Secondary Responsibilities: Perform other job-related duties as assigned and participate in the intellectual life of the Department, the College of Arts & Sciences, and the University. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS (education/training and experience required) : Required: PhD complete prior to Fall 2027. A JD degree alone is not sufficient. Strong communication and interpersonal skills. Experience and commitment to excellence in teaching at the college level. Commitment to equity, inclusion, and belonging in teaching and service. Degree must be from an accredited university or college. Preferred: PhD in Criminal Justice, Criminology, or a closely related field. PHYSICAL REQUIREMENTS AND/OR UNUSUAL HOURS: Must be available for in-person instruction. Must be able to sit for extended periods while working at a computer or grading papers, and the ability to lecture in a classroom. Must have the ability to speak clearly and audibly to deliver lectures, facilitate classroom discussions, and interact with students and colleagues. Must have the ability to move around campus to attend meetings and teach in different classrooms. Minimal lifting may be involved for tasks such as carrying materials to class or moving books. All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Widener University is committed to fostering an inclusive community in which faculty, staff, and students from a variety of backgrounds, cultures, and personal experiences are welcomed and can thrive. We are an equal opportunity employer and are committed to providing equal educational and employment opportunities for all persons without regard to age, color, national origin, race, religion, disability, veteran status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, or status as a protected veteran. U.S. work authorization is required. Widener University, an independent, metropolitan, doctoral-intensive university, connects curricula to social issues through civic engagement. Dynamic teaching, active scholarship, personal attention and experiential learning are key components of the Widener Experience. Located in Chester, PA, Widener's main campus is nestled between Philadelphia, PA and Wilmington, DE, with Law Schools located in both Harrisburg and Wilmington. For more information about the university, please visit our website at www.widener.edu. EOE M/F/V/D Advertised: 15 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.widener.edu/en-us/job/493435/admissions-counselor": {
      "title": "Admissions Counselor",
      "description": "Admissions Counselor Apply now Job no: 493435 Work type: Full-time (hourly) Location: Chester Categories: Staff Widener University is currently seeking an Admissions Counselor in Undergraduate Admissions. This role is responsible for assisting students with the admissions process and other relevant aspects of the University. This role reports to the Senior Associate Director of Admissions and supports the recruitment of new undergraduate first year domestic students. Admissions Counselors are enthusiastic, professional representatives of the university with a high interest in interpersonal communication and relationship building. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES (including, but not limited to) : Essential Duties: Supporting off-campus recruitment by representing the University at high school visits, college fairs and other external outreach events. Supporting on-campus recruitment by contributing to, open houses, workshops, campus tours and other events that showcase the campus and academic programs. Planning and assisting with virtual recruitment events including, but not limited to, online chats. Presenting and delivering engaging presentations for prospective students and families. Evaluate new student credentials and recommend admission decisions. Cultivate relationships with high school counselors and other key prospective student influencers. Plan and execute creative recruitment strategies within an assigned geographic territory and with support of Senior Associate Director of Admissions; travel extensively within assigned geographic territory. Remain informed and up to date on all Widener University recruitment messages, facts, and policies. Use Slate CRM to support recruitment, student tracking, and data retrieval. Be an active and positive member of the undergraduate admissions team, demonstrating a willingness to participate and volunteer in team initiatives. Secondary Responsibilities: Collaborate with other campus units to support recruitment of new students. Contribute creative ideas to the overall undergraduate recruitment strategy that meet the generational trends. Perform other job-related duties as assigned or directed to support departmental needs and align with the mission, values and goals of Undergraduate Admissions and Widener University. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS (education/training and experience required) : Required: Bachelor’s degree. Superior oral and written communication skills. Strong organizational skills, exceptional attention to detail and the ability to multi-task. Proficient in using Microsoft Suite (Word, Excel, PowerPoint). Preferred: Experience in contributing to a climate of inclusivity and working with a diverse student body. Career interest in admissions counseling and/or higher education. Bilingual with a preference for Spanish language skills. PHYSICAL REQUIREMENTS AND/OR UNUSUAL HOURS: Valid driver’s license and clear driving record. Evening and weekend work are required. Must be able to sit and work on a computer for 2+ hours minimum at a time. Must be able to stand and move between buildings at recruitment events, and provide walking tours, when necessary, across campus. Must be able to lift up to 25 lbs. All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Widener University is committed to fostering an inclusive community in which faculty, staff, and students from a variety of backgrounds, cultures, and personal experiences are welcomed and can thrive. We are an equal opportunity employer and are committed to providing equal educational and employment opportunities for all persons without regard to age, color, national origin, race, religion, disability, veteran status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, or status as a protected veteran. Some positions may be eligible for a hybrid or remote work arrangement that may include a partially or fully remote work location, consistent with Widener’s Flexible Work policy. Widener University, an independent, metropolitan, doctoral-intensive university, connects curricula to social issues through civic engagement. Dynamic teaching, active scholarship, personal attention, and experiential learning are key components of the Widener Experience. Located in Chester, PA, Widener's main campus is nestled between Philadelphia, PA and Wilmington, DE, with Law Schools located in both Harrisburg and Wilmington. For more information about the university, please visit our website at www.widener.edu. EOE M/F/V/D Advertised: 14 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.widener.edu/en-us/job/493436/assistant-professor-of-philosophy": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Philosophy",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Philosophy Apply now Job no: 493436 Work type: Faculty (Tenure track) Location: Chester Categories: Faculty Reporting to the Dean of the College of Arts & Sciences, the Assistant Professor of Philosophy is a full-time tenure-track position beginning in Fall 2027. The Department of Philosophy in Widener University's College of Arts and Sciences invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor of Philosophy beginning in Fall 2027. The successful candidate must be prepared to teach Symbolic Logic (sentential and first-order predicate logic) and introductory ethics. The ability to teach business ethics is a plus. The teaching load is 4-3. It will include regular sections of Symbolic Logic and ethics courses serving the University's general education program, as well as courses in the candidate's areas of expertise. Faculty may also teach ASC-101: Thinking Through, a course required of all incoming students in the University's Foundations and Transformations general education program, in which each section is built around a pressing question chosen by the instructor. Faculty may also contribute to the department's Ethical Reasoning certificate and minor. We seek a colleague committed to excellent undergraduate teaching who maintains an active scholarly agenda appropriate to a teaching-focused institution. Widener is a metropolitan university in Chester, Pennsylvania, near Philadelphia. Many of our students are first-generation college students, and many are preparing for careers in nursing, business, engineering, and other professional fields. Philosophy courses at Widener, particularly logic and ethics, serve students across the university, most of whom are not philosophy majors. We are looking for a teacher who finds that work meaningful and knows how to reach students who did not arrive expecting to study philosophy. The Ph.D. in Philosophy or a closely related field is required by August 15, 2027. Widener University is committed to fostering an environment in which faculty, staff, and students from a variety of backgrounds, cultures, and personal experiences are welcomed and can thrive. The College of Arts and Sciences puts high priority on inclusive pedagogy, inclusive mentoring, and community engagement. Candidates who have experience working with a diverse range of faculty, staff, and students, or who would enjoy it, and who can contribute to a climate of inclusivity, are encouraged to identify their experiences in these areas throughout their application. To apply: Complete the online application form at widener.edu/jobs. Submit the following as a single PDF: A cover letter that addresses three items: Your preparation to teach Symbolic Logic (e.g., prior teaching or TA experience in formal logic, or graduate coursework). Your preparation to teach introductory ethics, and business ethics if applicable. Your current and planned scholarship. A CV. A statement of teaching philosophy. Evidence of teaching effectiveness, consisting at minimum of course evaluations, one sample syllabus, and one sample assignment. Incomplete applications will not be considered. Longlisted candidates will be asked to arrange for three confidential letters of reference and to provide graduate transcripts. First-round interviews will be conducted via Zoom. Review of applications will begin on November 2, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. For more information about Widener University and the Department of Philosophy, see www.widener.edu . DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES (including, but not limited to) : Essential duties: Teaching Three or four classes a semester in Fall and Spring in area of expertise and/or first-year courses. Research Establish and maintain a successful research program in the candidate’s field. Academic Citizenship Participate in departmental faculty meetings focused on departmental operations and planning and faculty development, including pedagogical strategies and student support initiatives. Participate in humanities division, college, and university meetings focused on faculty governance, curricular development, and enhancing campus-wide inclusive excellence. Secondary Responsibilities: Perform other job-related duties as assigned and participation in the intellectual life of the Department, the College of Arts & Sciences, and the University. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS (education/training and experience required) : Required: A demonstrated commitment to excellent undergraduate teaching, as shown in teaching materials and evaluations. Competence to teach symbolic logic through first-order predicate logic, as shown by prior teaching or TA experience in formal logic, or graduate coursework. Ability to teach introductory ethics. An active scholarly agenda appropriate to a teaching-focused institution. A Ph.D. in Philosophy or a closely related field by August 15, 2027. Degree must be from an accredited university or college. Preferred: Ability to teach business ethics. Interest in interdisciplinary teaching, including ASC-101: Thinking Through, the University's required first-year course, in which each section is organized around a pressing question chosen by the instructor. PHYSICAL REQUIREMENTS AND/OR UNUSUAL HOURS: Must be available for in-person instruction. Must have the ability to speak clearly and audibly to deliver lectures, facilitate classroom discussions and interact with students and colleagues. All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Widener University is committed to fostering an inclusive community in which faculty, staff, and students from a variety of backgrounds, cultures, and personal experiences are welcomed and can thrive. We are an equal opportunity employer and are committed to providing equal educational and employment opportunities for all persons without regard to age, color, national origin, race, religion, disability, veteran status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, or status as a protected veteran. Some positions may be eligible for a hybrid or remote work arrangement that may include a partially or fully remote work location, consistent with Widener’s Flexible Work policy. U.S. work authorization is required. Widener University, an independent, metropolitan, doctoral-intensive university, connects curricula to social issues through civic engagement. Dynamic teaching, active scholarship, personal attention and experiential learning are key components of the Widener Experience. Located in Chester, PA, Widener's main campus is nestled between Philadelphia, PA and Wilmington, DE, with Law Schools located in both Harrisburg and Wilmington. For more information about the university, please visit our website at www.widener.edu. EOE M/F/V/D Advertised: 14 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.widener.edu/en-us/job/493433/assistant-professor-of-biology-cell-and-molecular-biology": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Biology - Cell and Molecular Biology",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Biology - Cell and Molecular Biology Apply now Job no: 493433 Work type: Faculty (Tenure track) Location: Chester Categories: Faculty Reporting to the Dean of the College of Arts & Sciences, the Assistant Professor of Biology is a full-time, tenure track position beginning in Fall 2027. The Department of Biology at Widener University seeks candidates with strong research and teaching experience in Cell and Molecular Biology. We welcome applicants with a broad array of backgrounds in cell and molecular biology research. The successful candidate will have a strong commitment to excellence in undergraduate teaching, and in mentoring and engaging undergraduate students in their research program. They will join a supportive environment in the Department of Biology with a history of close collaboration between non-tenure track teaching faculty and tenure-track faculty in teaching and scholarly pursuits, within and between the departments. A private office and research space where cell culture and molecular biology research can be conducted is available. Shared facilities include greenhouse space and access to Widener's arboretum, a staffed prep room, and multi-use laboratory spaces equipped for molecular biology and microbiology, plant and animal care, and microscopy. The Department of Biology is committed to fostering a sense of belonging in science. We comprise faculty, staff and students representing a wide range of backgrounds, cultures, and personal experiences. We seek candidates who aspire to create an environment of inclusive excellence where our students and our colleagues can thrive and achieve success in their chosen fields. Those candidates with experience in (or who would enjoy) working with a diverse range of faculty, staff and students, and who would contribute to the climate of inclusivity are encouraged to identify their experiences in these areas throughout their application. Review of application materials will begin October 5, 2026, and continue on a rolling basis until position is filled. Applicants must submit: a letter of application, their curriculum vitae, and statements of their teaching philosophy and research interests. Three confidential letters of reference and any additional related materials must also be sent to artsandsciences@widener.edu. In these materials, provide detailed examples of your experiences with diverse student populations and demonstrate how you could promote an environment of inclusive excellence in our department and in the broader Widener University community through your teaching, scholarship, and service activities. All documents must be submitted electronically (docx or pdf format). For more information about Widener University and the Department of Biology, see www.widener.edu . DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES (including, but not limited to) : Essential duties: Teaching Teach 21 credits/contact hours per year Teach lecture and laboratory sections for sophomore-level cell and molecular courses for Biology majors and non-majors. Design and teach upper-level cell and molecular biology elective courses for Biology majors. These courses may be specific to the candidate’s field or discipline. Maintain regular weekly office hours to provide academic support and guidance to students enrolled in assigned courses. Research Establish and maintain a successful research program in the candidate’s field or discipline Engage and mentor undergraduate students in their research program Institutional Engagement As Academic Advisor, guide and mentor assigned undergraduate students through the 4-year Biology curriculum sequence. Participate in weekly departmental faculty meetings focused on departmental operations and planning, faculty development, including pedagogical strategies and student support initiatives. Participate in science division, college, and university meetings focused on faculty governance, higher-level student and curricular development, and enhancing campus-wide inclusive excellence. Secondary Responsibilities: Perform other work-related duties as elected to support institutional needs that align with the mission of the Department of Biology, the College of Arts & Sciences, and the University. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS (education/training and experience required) : Required: Ph.D. in Biology (or related field) with a specialization in Cell and Molecular Biology Two or more years of post-doctoral experience is required Demonstrated experience in teaching and in engaging undergraduate students in research, specifically in cell and molecular biology. Pedagogical Commitment: Evidence of capacity and commitment to inclusive learning, active teaching strategies, and student-centered pedagogy, as demonstrated by experience, expertise, and/or formal training. Lab Instruction: Experience teaching introductory or upper-level biology laboratory courses. Degree must be from an accredited university or college. Preferred: 2–3 years of teaching experience at the college or university level. PHYSICAL REQUIREMENTS AND/OR UNUSUAL HOURS: Must be available for in-person instruction. Ability to move throughout laboratory spaces to supervise students. Must have the ability to move around campus to attend meetings and teach in different classrooms. Minimal lifting may be involved for tasks such as carrying materials to class All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Widener University is committed to fostering an inclusive community in which faculty, staff, and students from a variety of backgrounds, cultures, and personal experiences are welcomed and can thrive. We are an equal opportunity employer and are committed to providing equal educational and employment opportunities for all persons without regard to age, color, national origin, race, religion, disability, veteran status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, or status as a protected veteran. U.S. work authorization is required. Widener University, an independent, metropolitan, doctoral-intensive university, connects curricula to social issues through civic engagement. Dynamic teaching, active scholarship, personal attention and experiential learning are key components of the Widener Experience. Located in Chester, PA, Widener's main campus is nestled between Philadelphia, PA and Wilmington, DE, with Law Schools located in both Harrisburg and Wilmington. For more information about the university, please visit our website at www.widener.edu. EOE M/F/V/D Advertised: 11 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.widener.edu/en-us/job/493434/assistant-professor-of-biology-ecology-animal-physiology": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Biology - Ecology & Animal Physiology",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Biology - Ecology & Animal Physiology Apply now Job no: 493434 Work type: Faculty (Tenure track) Location: Chester Categories: Faculty Reporting to the Dean of the College of Arts & Sciences, the Assistant Professor of Biology is a full-time, tenure track position beginning in Fall 2027. The Department of Biology at Widener University seeks candidates with strong research and teaching experience in ecology and animal physiology. Candidates with research experience in any area of ecological focus (plant-animal, animal-microbe, etc.) are encouraged to apply, but the successful candidate must have teaching experience related to vertebrate or human physiology. Ideal candidates will have a strong commitment to excellence in undergraduate teaching, and in mentoring and engaging undergraduate students in their research program. The successful candidate will join a supportive environment in the Department of Biology, with potential to collaborate in both research and pedagogy within and across departments/disciplines at Widener. Facilities available include greenhouse space, Widener’s arboretum, a staffed prep room, individual office and laboratory space, and multi-use laboratory spaces equipped for molecular biology and microbiology, plant and animal care, and microscopy. The campus is close to diverse natural habitats including John Heinz National Wildlife Refuge at Tinicum, Ridley Creek State Park (PA), Alapocas Run State Park (DE), and the New Jersey Pine Barrens. The Department of Biology is committed to fostering a sense of belonging in science. We comprise faculty, staff, and students representing a wide range of backgrounds, cultures, and personal experiences. We seek candidates who aspire to create an environment of inclusive excellence where our students can thrive and achieve success in their chosen fields. Those candidates experienced in, or who would enjoy, working with a diverse range of faculty, staff, and students, and those who can contribute to the climate of inclusivity, are encouraged to identify their experiences in these areas throughout their application. Review of application materials will begin October 23 rd , 2026. Applicants must submit: a letter of application, a curriculum vitae, and a research statement, and a statement of teaching philosophy. In addition, three confidential letters of reference and any additional related materials must also be sent to artsandsciences@widener.edu. In these materials, provide detailed examples of your experiences with diverse student populations and demonstrate how you could promote an environment of inclusive excellence in our department and in the broader Widener University community through your teaching, scholarship, and service activities. All documents must be submitted electronically (docx or pdf format). For more information about Widener University and the Department of Biology, see www.widener.edu . DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES (including, but not limited to) : Essential duties: Teaching Teach 21 credits/contact hours per year Teach lecture and laboratory sections for introductory and upper-level Biology students and non-majors. Design and teach upper-level ecology and vertebrate or human physiology elective courses for Biology majors. These courses may be specific to the candidate’s field or discipline. Maintain regular weekly office hours to provide academic support and guidance to students enrolled in assigned courses. Research Establish and maintain a successful research program in the candidate’s field or discipline Engage and mentor undergraduate students in their research program Institutional Engagement As an academic advisor, guide and mentor assigned undergraduate students through the 4-year Biology curriculum sequence. Participate in weekly departmental faculty meetings focused on departmental operations and planning, faculty development, including pedagogical strategies and student support initiatives. Participate in science division, college, and university meetings focused on faculty governance, higher-level student and curricular development, and enhancing campus-wide inclusive excellence. Secondary Responsibilities: Perform other work-related duties as elected to support institutional needs that align with the mission of the Department of Biology, the College of Arts & Sciences, and the University. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS (education/training and experience required) : Required: Ph.D. in Biology (or related field) Teaching experience in an upper-level Vertebrate or Human Physiology course Demonstrated experience in teaching and engaging undergraduate students in research, specifically in ecology and/or physiology. Pedagogical Commitment: Evidence of capacity and commitment to inclusive learning, active teaching strategies, and student-centered pedagogy, as demonstrated by experience, expertise, and/or formal training. Lab Instruction: Experience teaching introductory or upper-level biology laboratory courses. Degree must be from an accredited university or college. Preferred: Two or more years of teaching experience at the college or university level. Two or more years of post-doctoral experience. PHYSICAL REQUIREMENTS AND/OR UNUSUAL HOURS: Must be available for in-person instruction. Ability to move throughout laboratory spaces to supervise students. Must have the ability to move around campus to attend meetings and teach in different classrooms. Minimal lifting may be involved for tasks such as carrying materials to class All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Widener University is committed to fostering an inclusive community in which faculty, staff, and students from a variety of backgrounds, cultures, and personal experiences are welcomed and can thrive. We are an equal opportunity employer and are committed to providing equal educational and employment opportunities for all persons without regard to age, color, national origin, race, religion, disability, veteran status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, or status as a protected veteran. U.S. work authorization is required. Widener University, an independent, metropolitan, doctoral-intensive university, connects curricula to social issues through civic engagement. Dynamic teaching, active scholarship, personal attention and experiential learning are key components of the Widener Experience. Located in Chester, PA, Widener's main campus is nestled between Philadelphia, PA and Wilmington, DE, with Law Schools located in both Harrisburg and Wilmington. For more information about the university, please visit our website at www.widener.edu. EOE M/F/V/D Advertised: 11 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.widener.edu/en-us/job/493429/advancement-constituent-relationship-management-crm-administrator": {
      "title": "Advancement Constituent Relationship Management (CRM) Administrator",
      "description": "Advancement Constituent Relationship Management (CRM) Administrator Apply now Job no: 493429 Work type: Full-time Location: Chester Categories: Administrators As a member of University Advancement's Development and Engagement team, the Advancement CRM Administrator reports to the Executive Director of Annual Giving and Advancement Services and serves as the primary administrator and strategic steward of the Constituent Relationship Management (CRM) system that supports fundraising, alumni engagement, and donor stewardship efforts. This position is responsible for the administration, optimization, and ongoing evolution of the CRM and related advancement technologies. The Administrator ensures that systems, data, and processes effectively support advancement goals while maintaining data integrity, security, and compliance with institutional standards. By partnering with Advancement staff, University IT, and external vendors, this role helps drive operational efficiency, support data-informed decision making, and enhance the user experience across the advancement technology ecosystem. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES (including, but not limited to) : CRM Administration & Technology Strategy Serve as the primary administrator for the Advancement CRM, supporting system functionality, user needs, and ongoing enhancements. Partner with University Advancement and University IT leadership to implement the advancement technology roadmap and align systems with evolving fundraising and engagement priorities. Evaluate and recommend system enhancements, new features, and third-party tools to improve operational effectiveness. Support system upgrades, testing efforts, and implementation of new functionality. Systems Integration & Data Management Manage and support integrations between the CRM and related advancement and university systems. Oversee data imports, exports, and automated processes to ensure accurate and consistent information across platforms. Establish and maintain data standards, business rules, and quality control procedures that promote data integrity and reliable reporting. Manage user security and access permissions in accordance with institutional policies and data privacy standards. Project Management & Collaboration Lead and support CRM-related projects from planning and requirements gathering through implementation and user adoption. Collaborate with Advancement stakeholders to develop system solutions, reports, dashboards, and workflows that support fundraising, engagement, and operational goals. Prioritize system enhancement requests and communicate project status and outcomes to key stakeholders. Develop and maintain documentation, training materials, and resources that support effective system use. User Support & Continuous Improvement Provide technical guidance, troubleshooting, and training for CRM users. Partner with Advancement Services colleagues to support business processes, including fundraising operations and gift processing. Identify opportunities to improve processes through automation, technology enhancements, and emerging tools. Evaluate opportunities for automation, AI-enabled functionality, and emerging technologies while ensuring appropriate data governance, privacy security, and human oversight. Perform other job-related duties as assigned in alignment with Widener University’s mission, values, and fundraising priorities. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS (education/training and experience required) : Required Bachelor's degree in Business, Information Systems, Data Science, or a related field, or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Three (3+) years of experience administering and supporting CRM or enterprise database systems, preferably in higher education, advancement, or nonprofit organizations. Experience managing constituent or customer data, system configurations, reporting, and user support. Demonstrated ability to maintain data integrity through data governance, quality control, and record management practices. Strong project management, analytical, problem-solving, and organizational skills. Excellent communication and customer service skills, with the ability to explain technical concepts to non-technical users. Preferred Experience with Blackbaud NXT, Slate, Salesforce, or similar CRM platforms. Familiarity with advancement technologies such as AlumniQ, ThankView/EverTrue, or Colleague. Experience supporting CRM implementations, system integrations, or major technology enhancement projects. Experience working with external vendors and technology partners. PHYSICAL REQUIREMENTS AND/OR UNUSUAL HOURS: Ability and willingness to travel to ensure successful volunteer engagement and cultivation activities. Ability and willingness to work evenings and weekends required. Ability to stand for long periods of time at campus events, presentations or other related events. All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Widener University is committed to fostering an inclusive community in which faculty, staff, and students from a variety of backgrounds, cultures, and personal experiences are welcomed and can thrive. We are an equal opportunity employer and are committed to providing equal educational and employment opportunities for all persons without regard to age, color, national origin, race, religion, disability, veteran status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, or status as a protected veteran. Some positions may be eligible for a hybrid or remote work arrangement that may include a partially or fully remote work location, consistent with Widener’s Flexible Work policy. Widener University, an independent, metropolitan, doctoral-intensive university, connects curricula to social issues through civic engagement. Dynamic teaching, active scholarship, personal attention and experiential learning are key components of the Widener Experience. Located in Chester, PA, Widener's main campus is nestled between Philadelphia, PA and Wilmington, DE, with Law Schools located in both Harrisburg and Wilmington. For more information about the university, please visit our website at www.widener.edu. EOE M/F/V/D Advertised: 09 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.widener.edu/en-us/job/493431/assistant-professor-of-clinical-psychology": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Clinical Psychology",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Clinical Psychology Apply now Job no: 493431 Work type: Faculty (Tenure track) Location: Chester Categories: Faculty Widener University is currently seeking applications for an Assistant Professor of Clinical Psychology - Tenure Track in the College of Health and Human Services, to start in the Fall of 2027. This ten-month position is a core faculty position in our graduate level Doctor of Psychology (PsyD) program. We are a generalist clinical program and our faculty represents a diverse array of theoretical orientations. We encourage applications from candidates representing any theoretical orientations who, in addition to their areas of individual scholarly/clinical specialty, can teach core courses in a generalist clinical psychology curriculum (e.g., foundational courses, statistics/research methods, intervention, assessment, diversity and multicultural theory). All candidates should be prepared to demonstrate a sophisticated understanding of diversity, equity, inclusion, and belonging, as well as how this understanding is woven into their work. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES (including, but not limited to) : Essential duties: Teaching: Teach 4 courses per year in doctoral level clinical psychology program, chair 2-4 dissertations per year and serve as dissertation committee member for 3-4 students per year; provide advising/mentorship to students. Scholarship: Engage in scholarly work related to area(s) of interest, and demonstrate scholarship through presentations and/or publications in peer-reviewed journals. Service: Attend and participate in faculty meetings; serve in faculty governance/committee work as assigned/elected at the program, College, and University level; participate in unit admissions reviews; provide service to the profession via membership in professional organizations and participation in professional/community-based service activities. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS (education/training and experience required) : Required: Psy.D. or Ph.D. in clinical or counseling psychology. ABD candidates may be considered if the doctoral degree will be completed by August 2027. Demonstrated commitment to graduate education. PA license as a psychologist. Supervision toward licensure can be provided to qualified applicants who have completed all other Pennsylvania requirements for licensure as a psychologist. Clear evidence of scholarly potential. Position is not limited by area of research specialization. Evidence of clear integration of issues related to diversity, equity, inclusion, and belonging into clinical work, research, and/or teaching. Degree must be from an accredited university or college. Preferred: Teaching experience in Psychology in higher education. Experience providing clinical supervision. Qualified candidates must submit a resume and cover letter to be considered. Candidate review will remain open until position filled, although priority consideration will be given to applications received by November 15, 2026. Incomplete applications will not be considered. Only those selected to interview will be contacted. All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Widener University is committed to fostering an inclusive community in which faculty, staff, and students from a variety of backgrounds, cultures, and personal experiences are welcomed and can thrive. We are an equal opportunity employer and are committed to providing equal educational and employment opportunities for all persons without regard to age, color, national origin, race, religion, disability, veteran status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, or status as a protected veteran. U.S. work authorization is required. Widener University, an independent, metropolitan, doctoral-intensive university, connects curricula to social issues through civic engagement. Dynamic teaching, active scholarship, personal attention and experiential learning are key components of the Widener Experience. Located in Chester, PA, Widener's main campus is nestled between Philadelphia, PA and Wilmington, DE, with Law Schools located in both Harrisburg and Wilmington. For more information about the university, please visit our website at www.widener.edu. EOE M/F/V/D Advertised: 09 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.widener.edu/en-us/job/493428/state-grant-coordinator": {
      "title": "State Grant Coordinator",
      "description": "State Grant Coordinator Apply now Job no: 493428 Work type: Full-time (hourly) Location: Chester Categories: Staff The State Grant Coordinator is responsible for administering state grants, tuition benefit programs, and designated university funding programs while ensuring compliance with federal, state, and institutional regulations. This position maintains accurate student account records, reconciles funding and grant rosters, and submits timely and accurate reports to state agencies and other program stakeholders. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES (including, but not limited to) : Essential Duties: Initiate, review, update and reconcile student accounts for state grants, tuition benefits programs, and some Widener University funds. Reconcile all state grant rosters and other funding, as required. Submit timely and accurate reports to state agencies. Stay current with regulations to maintain compliance with a variety of state grant and special programs. Advise students, staff, and certain special program participants regarding financial status processes, procedures, and requirements and advise on alternative courses of action and consequences. Responsible for the administration of PA and other state grants, tuition benefit programs and some Widener University funds. Monitor and respond to general inquiries in the Financial Aid email account. Provide assistance on the phone que with transactions and inquiries regarding Financial Aid, Bursar, Registrar and One Card offices. Answer a wide variety of questions posed by potential and current students, staff, faculty and visitors. Secondary Responsibilities: Understand the basic functions of Financial Aid, Bursar, Registrar and One Card offices as a representative in the enrollment services and one stop area. Instruct students on web-based transactions; resolve holds on student records. Perform other job-related duties as assigned or directed to support departmental needs and align with the mission, values and goals of Student Financial Services and Widener University. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS (education/training and experience required) : Required: High School diploma or equivalent. Focus on details of position. Ability to read and follow oral and written instructions. Ability to utilize a computer/laptop, applicable software, email and internet. Experience with the Microsoft Office suite (Outlook, Excel, Word). Must be organized, flexible and able to handle multiple tasks simultaneously. Must have interpersonal skills and excellent oral and written communication skills. Have the ability to interact effectively and with good customer service skills with students, staff, faculty and external constituents. Preferred: Associate’s degree . Experience using Ellucian Colleague. Customer Service. PA State Grant and/or Special Program experience. PHYSICAL REQUIREMENTS AND/OR UNUSUAL HOURS: Be able to sit and use computer for extended periods of time. May be asked to work with team members on occasional weekends. All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Widener University is committed to fostering an inclusive community in which faculty, staff, and students from a variety of backgrounds, cultures, and personal experiences are welcomed and can thrive. We are an equal opportunity employer and are committed to providing equal educational and employment opportunities for all persons without regard to age, color, national origin, race, religion, disability, veteran status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, or status as a protected veteran. Some positions may be eligible for a hybrid or remote work arrangement that may include a partially or fully remote work location, consistent with Widener’s Flexible Work policy. Widener University, an independent, metropolitan, doctoral-intensive university, connects curricula to social issues through civic engagement. Dynamic teaching, active scholarship, personal attention and experiential learning are key components of the Widener Experience. Located in Chester, PA, Widener's main campus is nestled between Philadelphia, PA and Wilmington, DE, with Law Schools located in both Harrisburg and Wilmington. For more information about the university, please visit our website at www.widener.edu. EOE M/F/V/D Advertised: 03 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.widener.edu/en-us/job/493422/assistant-director-of-financial-aid-delaware-law-school": {
      "title": "Assistant Director of Financial Aid -Delaware Law School",
      "description": "Assistant Director of Financial Aid -Delaware Law School Apply now Job no: 493422 Work type: Full-time Location: Delaware Categories: Administrators Widener University is currently seeking an Assistant Director of Financial Aid at the Delaware Law School. This role reports to the Director of Financial Aid and provides support to the Division of Enrollment Office (Bursar/Financial Aid) of Delaware Law School. Responsible for managing the daily functioning of the Financial Aid Office on the Delaware campus, the individual will have system management experience with a highly automated financial aid management system. The individual will assist the Director in the development of policies and procedures and will have significant knowledge of the overall operation of the Financial Aid function of the School of Law. The Assistant Director assists the Director in representing the office to external constituents. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES (including, but not limited to) : Essential Duties: Ensures the effective operation of the financial aid office by utilizing knowledge and expertise in office procedures and systems management and applying that knowledge to the operation of the automated financial aid management system including the Communications module, Direct Loan and ISIR processing and the Student Financial Aid module of Ellucian Colleague. Analyzes and processes student application files ensuring compliance with Federal, State and institutional requirements. Determines award eligibility for financial aid including Federal and private education loans and University based aid. Ensures compliance with state and federal regulations by assisting in the preparation of required reports concerning financial aid: reconciles Pennsylvania and Delaware State Grant Rosters, monitors reports of Federal Work Study earnings for all programs and Federal Pell grants for Legal Studies students. Counsels prospective students, and families on financial aid and financing alternatives. Represents the financial aid office at Recruitment events. Assists with federally mandated group entrance and exit counseling for incoming and graduating students. Calculates Return of Title IV funds ensuring compliance with Federal regulations and applies institutional policy for withdrawn students. Ensures compliance and stays current with Federal regulations pertaining to Financial Aid administration. Maintains contact with other institutions to complete consortium agreements. Secondary Responsibilities: Provides backup support to the Division of Enrollment Representative managing front line tasks such as phones, walk-in traffic and forms intake. Engages with other offices (Admissions, Registrar, Payroll) to ensure smooth flow of communication and information. Assists with the review and request updates of the department's website content and financial aid forms available for download for students. Assists with research and creation of student cost of attendance and budgets based on federal regulations and best practices. Assists with administration of the Widener Scholar Loan program as needed. Perform other job-related duties as assigned or directed to support departmental needs and align with the mission, values and goals of the Delaware Law School Financial Aid Office and Widener University. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS (education/training and experience required) : Required: Bachelor’s degree. Four (4+) years of progressive financial aid experience demonstrating a broad knowledge in a specialized field directly related to the position’s responsibilities. An equivalent combination of education and experience may be considered. One (1) year progressively responsible managerial/supervisory experience with exposure to budget management, strategic planning and human resource management. Extensive knowledge of federal and state laws and regulations that govern student financial aid. Demonstrated ability to utilize a personal computer, applicable software, e-mail and the Internet. Experience in the use of an administrative database system in the administration of a financial aid program. Demonstrated ability to interact effectively with students, support and professional staff, faculty, and external constituents. Must be organized, flexible, and able to handle several tasks simultaneously. Superior interpersonal skills and excellent oral and written communication skills. Preferred: Experience in utilizing an administrative database (Ellucian Colleague). PHYSICAL REQUIREMENTS AND/OR UNUSUAL HOURS: Ability to occasionally lift up to 20 pounds. Requires occasional evening and weekend hours. Possess a valid driver’s license. Ability to be mobile between workstation and various campus buildings and locations. Requires domestic travel. All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Widener University is committed to fostering an inclusive community in which faculty, staff, and students from a variety of backgrounds, cultures, and personal experiences are welcomed and can thrive. We are an equal opportunity employer and are committed to providing equal educational and employment opportunities for all persons without regard to age, color, national origin, race, religion, disability, veteran status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, or status as a protected veteran. Some positions may be eligible for a hybrid or remote work arrangement that may include a partially or fully remote work location, consistent with Widener’s Flexible Work policy. Widener University, an independent, metropolitan, doctoral-intensive university, connects curricula to social issues through civic engagement. Dynamic teaching, active scholarship, personal attention, and experiential learning are key components of the Widener Experience. Located in Chester, PA, Widener's main campus is nestled between Philadelphia, PA and Wilmington, DE, with Law Schools located in both Harrisburg and Wilmington. For more information about the university, please visit our website at www.widener.edu. EOE M/F/V/D Advertised: 18 Aug 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.widener.edu/en-us/job/493389/campus-safety-officer-1st-shift": {
      "title": "Campus Safety Officer, 1st Shift",
      "description": "Campus Safety Officer, 1st Shift Apply now Job no: 493389 Work type: Full-time (hourly) Location: Chester Categories: Staff Widener University is currently seeking a full-time 1 st Shift Campus Safety Officer. This position works both indoors and outdoors, averaging 40 hours per week, with shifts from 8:00 AM to 4:00 PM. The position reports directly to the on-duty Lieutenant. Campus Safety Officers are responsible for responding promptly and effectively to calls for service, protecting University property and assets, enforcing University policies and regulations, and promoting positive community engagement through respectful, customer-focused interactions. Reporting directly to the Campus Safety Lieutenant, the Campus Safety Officer plays a vital role in maintaining a safe, secure, and welcoming environment for all members of the University community. This full-time role averages 40 hours per week, with scheduled hours from 8:00 AM to 4:00 PM, and includes both indoor and outdoor responsibilities. This position requires a strong commitment to public safety, excellent communication skills, and the ability to build trust across a diverse campus population. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES (including, but not limited to) : Essential Duties: Serve as a designated Campus Security Authority (CSA) in compliance with the Clery Act. Conduct proactive, high-visibility patrols by foot, vehicle, or other means to deter and detect security threats. Respond promptly and professionally to service calls; conduct initial investigations and resolve incidents. Address reports of fire hazards, mechanical malfunctions, altercations, accidents, and criminal activity. Inspect campus facilities to identify and report potential security vulnerabilities. De-escalate conflicts and disputes with professionalism and composure. Accurately document incidents occurring on or near campus in detailed, objective reports. Provide courteous assistance and serve as a point of contact for campus inquiries and concerns. Collaborate with local law enforcement and emergency responders when necessary. Secondary Responsibilities: Work flexible or additional shifts, including dispatch duties as needed. Support University athletic events, ceremonies, and special functions. Monitor and manage crowd control during peak times and large gatherings. Report maintenance issues such as broken locks, lighting failures, or other safety concerns. Assist in coordinating emergency response efforts with external agencies during critical incidents. Perform other job-related duties as assigned or directed to support departmental needs and align with the mission, values and goals of Campus Safety. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS (education/training and experience required) : Required: High school diploma or equivalent. Valid driver’s license. Prior experience in customer service or a security-related role. Strong interpersonal skills and ability to engage professionally with diverse populations. Proficiency in operating computers, radios, and mobile or vehicle-based equipment. Excellent verbal and written communication skills. Ability to work independently and collaboratively within a team. Demonstrated ability to remain calm and tactful in challenging situations. Successful completion of background checks including PA Criminal, PA Child Abuse, and FBI clearance. Preferred: Experience in higher education or school-based environments. Background in law enforcement, military, campus or public safety. Familiarity with the local community and surrounding areas. PHYSICAL REQUIREMENTS AND/OR UNUSUAL HOURS: Ability to walk for extended periods and navigate stairs in various building environments. Capacity to patrol outdoors in all weather conditions. Ability to stand or sit for long durations, both indoors and outdoors. Must be able to lift and carry objects weighing up to 55 lbs. Willingness to work non-standard hours, including nights, weekends, and holidays as needed. All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Widener University is committed to fostering an inclusive community in which faculty, staff, and students from a variety of backgrounds, cultures, and personal experiences are welcomed and can thrive. We are an equal opportunity employer and are committed to providing equal educational and employment opportunities for all persons without regard to age, color, national origin, race, religion, disability, veteran status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, or status as a protected veteran. Widener University, an independent, metropolitan, doctoral-intensive university, connects curricula to social issues through civic engagement. Dynamic teaching, active scholarship, personal attention and experiential learning are key components of the Widener Experience. Located in Chester, PA, Widener's main campus is nestled between Philadelphia, PA and Wilmington, DE, with Law Schools located in both Harrisburg and Wilmington. For more information about the university, please visit our website at www.widener.edu. EOE M/F/V/D Advertised: 17 Aug 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.widener.edu/en-us/job/493423/student-records-representative": {
      "title": "Student Records Representative",
      "description": "Student Records Representative Apply now Job no: 493423 Work type: Full-time (hourly) Location: Chester Categories: Staff Widener University is currently seeking a Student Records Representative. Reporting to the Assistant Registrar, the Student Records Representative serves as the primary point of contact for students, faculty, and staff, providing professional and responsive support through phone, email, and other electronic communications. This role is responsible for the daily management of the Registrar’s Office’s shared electronic inbox, ensuring timely review, processing, and resolution of records-related requests, inquiries, and transactions. The Student Records Representative maintains accurate student records, assists with administrative processes, and delivers high-quality customer service while ensuring compliance with University policies, procedures, and confidentiality standards. This position is responsible for performing a variety of administrative tasks and providing comprehensive support to the Registrar’s Office and Student Financial Services. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES (including, but not limited to) : Essential Duties: Registrar Documentation Processing Manage the intake and processing of all registrar-related documentation received electronically or in hard copy. Handle forms related to student registration, grade changes, program additions or modifications, and personal information updates. Ensure that all documents are complete, accurate, and processed in a timely manner. Registrar Email Management Serve as the primary manager of the Registrar’s Office email account. Monitor, prioritize, and respond to high volumes of inquiries from students, faculty, staff, and external stakeholders. Ensure timely and professional communication, escalating issues when necessary. File Maintenance and Organization Oversee the organization of both digital and physical registrar files. Ensure records are filed accurately, comply with institutional and legal standards, and are accessible for audits or reference. Cross-Departmental Frontline Support Log into the Enrollment telephone queue daily. Respond to and resolve inquiries related to Financial Aid, Bursar, Registrar, and OneCard services. Provide accurate information or triage inquiries for appropriate follow-up and resolution. Mail Intake and Transcript Entry Open, sort, and distribute all incoming mail addressed to the Registrar’s Office. Enter external transcript data into student records and coordinate with academic departments for evaluation and transfer of credit application. Degree and Enrollment Verifications Respond to degree and enrollment verification requests via the National Student Clearinghouse. Ensure compliance with FERPA and institutional policies when releasing student information. Document Imaging and Record Digitization Perform digital imaging and archival of Registrar’s Office documents. Support a transition to paperless operations and contribute to the improvement of records management systems. Ensure secure and efficient access to student records. Diploma Fulfillment Process and mail replacement diploma orders. Verify student eligibility and ensure the secure handling of academic credentials. Registrar Services Backup Support Provide reliable backup support for transcript processing and transfer credit evaluation. Maintain current knowledge of relevant systems and procedures to ensure uninterrupted registrar operations. Institutional Support and Administrative Flexibility Participate in cross-functional projects, campus events, and continuous improvement initiatives. Demonstrate adaptability, initiative, and commitment to service excellence within the Registrar’s Office. Perform other job-related duties as assigned or directed to support departmental needs and align with the mission, values and goals of Enrollment, Registrar’s Office and Widener University. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS (education/training and experience required) : Required: High School Diploma or equivalent. Three (3+) years of customer service experience in an office environment. Excellent communication and organizational skills. Attention to detail. Must possess a high level of diplomacy, tact and confidentiality. Proficient in the use of Microsoft Office Suite (Word, Excel, PowerPoint). Preferred: Previous work experience in a higher education environment. PHYSICAL REQUIREMENTS AND/OR UNUSUAL HOURS: May sit in front of a computer for long periods of time. Occasional work on weekends and evenings as required. All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Widener University is committed to fostering an inclusive community in which faculty, staff, and students from a variety of backgrounds, cultures, and personal experiences are welcomed and can thrive. We are an equal opportunity employer and are committed to providing equal educational and employment opportunities for all persons without regard to age, color, national origin, race, religion, disability, veteran status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, or status as a protected veteran. Some positions may be eligible for a hybrid or remote work arrangement that may include a partially or fully remote work location, consistent with Widener’s Flexible Work policy. Widener University, an independent, metropolitan, doctoral-intensive university, connects curricula to social issues through civic engagement. Dynamic teaching, active scholarship, personal attention, and experiential learning are key components of the Widener Experience. Located in Chester, PA, Widener's main campus is nestled between Philadelphia, PA and Wilmington, DE, with Law Schools located in both Harrisburg and Wilmington. For more information about the university, please visit our website at www.widener.edu. EOE M/F/V/D Advertised: 17 Aug 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.widener.edu/en-us/job/493421/financial-aid-counselor": {
      "title": "Financial Aid Counselor",
      "description": "Financial Aid Counselor Apply now Job no: 493421 Work type: Full-time (hourly) Location: Chester Categories: Staff Widener University is currently seeking a Financial Aid Counselor in the Student Financial Services department. Reporting to the Senior Associate Director of Financial Aid Services, the Financial Aid Counselor is responsible for awarding and processing undergraduate student financial aid; counsel students and families about financial aid options, policies and procedures; and package specialized population cohorts as assigned. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES (including, but not limited to ): Essential Duties: • Prepare financial aid offers for undergraduate students, resolve eligibility discrepancies; perform tax verification, process loans. • Counsel students, parents, and administrators regarding financial aid, payment plans, bills, and financing alternatives. • Make presentations to the general public about financial aid. • Utilize Ellucian/Colleague for packaging and access to other online federal/state agencies for loan certification and precertification. • Utilize FAA access, COD and the NSLDS to review and update student information. • Review documentation for integrity and determine institutional, state, and federal aid eligibility. • Implement, understand, and interpret federal, state, and institutional guidelines, and procedures to assist students in obtaining financial aid. • Act as liaison to Specific Programs at the University and externally. Secondary Responsibilities: • Recognize that decisions typically may influence multiple functions across the University but they do not carry long-term strategic implications. • Tasks which are moderately complex and varied require sound analytical skills. Routine decisions are expected, with limited alternative actions available. Supervisory support is accessible for non-standard issues. • Regularly handles sensitive and confidential information with discretion and professionalism. • Fully accountable for your own work. May assist in onboarding new employees or respond to inquiries directly related to the role. • Requires general awareness of upcoming projects and responsibilities, involving moderate levels of planning and coordination. • Maintain regular contact outside the department and University, primarily for routine information exchange. • Operates in a busy office setting with frequent walk-in traffic and a high level of interpersonal interaction, both in person and via phone or electronic communication. • Must be able to move freely between workstations, campus buildings, and various locations within the building. • Perform other job-related duties as assigned or directed to support departmental needs and align with the mission, values and goals of Student Financial Services and Widener University. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS (education/training and experience required): Required: • Bachelor's degree in Finance, Accounting, Education, Public Administration or a related area. An equivalent combination of education and experience may be considered. • Ability to read, understand, and follow both oral and written instructions. • Proficient in composing routine business correspondence with clarity and professionalism. • Capable of performing basic mathematical computations accurately. PHYSICAL REQUIREMENTS AND/OR UNUSUAL HOURS: • May require working occasional evenings and scheduled weekends to meet operational needs. All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Widener University is committed to fostering an inclusive community in which faculty, staff, and students from a variety of backgrounds, cultures, and personal experiences are welcomed and can thrive. We are an equal opportunity employer and are committed to providing equal educational and employment opportunities for all persons without regard to age, color, national origin, race, religion, disability, veteran status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, or status as a protected veteran. Some positions may be eligible for a hybrid or remote work arrangement that may include a partially or fully remote work location, consistent with Widener’s Flexible Work policy. Widener University, an independent, metropolitan, doctoral-intensive university, connects curricula to social issues through civic engagement. Dynamic teaching, active scholarship, personal attention, and experiential learning are key components of the Widener Experience. Located in Chester, PA, Widener's main campus is nestled between Philadelphia, PA and Wilmington, DE, with Law Schools located in both Harrisburg and Wilmington. For more information about the university, please visit our website at www.widener.edu . EOE M/F/V/D Advertised: 11 Aug 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.widener.edu/en-us/job/493387/staff-attorney": {
      "title": "Staff Attorney",
      "description": "Staff Attorney Apply now Job no: 493387 Work type: Full-time Location: Delaware Categories: Administrators Widener University is currently seeking a Staff Attorney for its Philadelphia site of the Health, Education, and Legal Assistance Project: A Medical -Legal Partnership at Delaware Law School (HELP: MLP). This is a hybrid position in which the Staff Attorney will work remotely as well as in our West Philadelphia office. Reporting to the Director and Managing Attorney, this grant-funded Staff Attorney role will be responsible for providing civil legal assistance to families with low incomes participating in maternal and child health programs. This role will work as part of an interdisciplinary team that supports families by coordinating direct legal services to clients, as well as providing training and education to public health partners, and engaging in systemic advocacy activities. HELP: MLP provides legal services to support families during pregnancy and early childhood, both critical periods when health-harming legal needs seriously impact maternal and infant health. We embed our staff within public health organizations, including nationally recognized home-visiting programs, such as Nurse-Family Partnership and Healthy Start. Together, we form medical-legal partnerships aimed at reducing the health inequities and racial disparities that negatively impact many of our client families. By working side-by-side with community-based home-visiting programs to screen for social determinants of health, our team can identify emerging legal needs early and take a preventative approach to legal services, to reduce family stress and foster the stability families need to thrive. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES (including, but not limited to) : Essential Duties: Provide direct legal services to clients, including litigation of landlord/tenant, public benefits, and other matters. Develop and deliver trainings to public health and community partners. Develop educational materials for public health partners and clients, including fact sheets, checklists, and handouts. Manage and organize client information using forms and an electronic legal case management system. Perform legal research and writing. Engage in systemic advocacy efforts to advance policy goals and improve community-wide health. Participate in local, state, and national coalitions and workgroups. Support the development of grant applications, reports, newsletters, articles, and other written publications. Local travel as needed to see clients and participate in work-related activities. Become and remain well-informed regarding basic legal principles applicable to area(s) of practice, attending training conferences as needed or requested by supervising attorney(s). Other duties as assigned. Secondary Responsibilities: Provide supervision and mentorship to law students as needed. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS (education/training and experience required) : Required: Admission to the Pennsylvania Bar. If not admitted in Pennsylvania, must be eligible to take the next scheduled bar examination and pass it. Excellent oral and written communication skills. Excellent organizational and time management skills. Ability to apply modern technology to performance of legal work and tasks, including keeping updated case notes in an electronic case management database. Contribute to the development of grant applications, reports, newsletters, articles, and other written publications. Must be willing to travel within the Philadelphia area as needed. Commitment to improving the lives of families with low incomes. Willingness and ability to perform duties in an interdisciplinary setting with public health partners. Flexibility, including willingness to work in any of HELP: MLP’s areas of practice as priorities may change based on clients’ needs or funding. Preferred: Verbal and written fluency in Spanish. At least 3 years of legal services in one of the following areas: public benefits, housing law, or family law. Lived experience similar to clients served, including lived experience of poverty and other forms of social adversity and challenges. Familiarity and/or experience of Philadelphia legal networks. Familiarity with the social services and community resources available in Philadelphia and how to access them. PHYSICAL REQUIREMENTS AND/OR UNUSUAL HOURS: Legal services at HELP: MLP are delivered at two sites - one in Eddystone, Pa. and one in Philadelphia, Pa. This position is located in West Philadelphia; however, the Staff Attorney will have a hybrid schedule of remote and on-site work. All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Widener University is committed to fostering an inclusive community in which faculty, staff, and students from a variety of backgrounds, cultures, and personal experiences are welcomed and can thrive. We are an equal opportunity employer and are committed to providing equal educational and employment opportunities for all persons without regard to age, color, national origin, race, religion, disability, veteran status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, or status as a protected veteran. Some positions may be eligible for a hybrid or remote work arrangement that may include a partially or fully remote work location, consistent with Widener’s Flexible Work policy. Widener University, an independent, metropolitan, doctoral-intensive university, connects curricula to social issues through civic engagement. Dynamic teaching, active scholarship, personal attention and experiential learning are key components of the Widener Experience. Located in Chester, PA, Widener's main campus is nestled between Philadelphia, PA and Wilmington, DE, with Law Schools located in both Harrisburg and Wilmington. For more information about the university, please visit our website at www.widener.edu. EOE M/F/V/D Advertised: 02 Jun 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.widener.edu/en-us/job/493388/adjunct-sociology": {
      "title": "Adjunct, Sociology",
      "description": "Adjunct, Sociology Apply now Job no: 493388 Work type: Adjunct Location: Chester Categories: Adjunct Widener University’s Sociology Department in the College of Arts and Sciences is currently seeking highly qualified adjunct faculty to teach in person Sociology courses. Part-time adjunct faculty may instruct 1 to 3 courses each semester on an “as needed” basis. Courses available for adjunct staffing are offered during the day. Adjunct faculty are expected to teach a specified course(s); prepare course materials, be available to meet with students outside of regularly scheduled class time, and provide support and guidance to students. For further information about the Department of Sociology please visit our website . Required qualifications include a minimum of a Master’s Degree in Sociology. Preference will be given to candidates who have ABD or PhD in Sociology, previous teaching experience, and willingness to be flexible in class scheduling. Complete applications are to include a cover letter, CV and contact information for three professional references. This is a pooled position from which adjunct(s) will be hired. As openings occur, applicants will be hired from this pool. All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Widener University is committed to fostering an inclusive community in which faculty, staff, and students from a variety of backgrounds, cultures, and personal experiences are welcomed and can thrive. We are an equal opportunity employer and are committed to providing equal educational and employment opportunities for all persons without regard to age, color, national origin, race, religion, disability, veteran status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, or status as a protected veteran. Wi