    "https://careers.widener.edu/en-us/job/493455/assistant-professor-of-law-commonwealth-law-school": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Law, Commonwealth Law School",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Law, Commonwealth Law School Apply now Job no: 493455 Work type: Faculty (Tenure track) Location: Harrisburg Categories: Faculty Widener Law Commonwealth seeks to hire entry-level or pre-tenure lateral faculty members to fill one or more tenure track positions starting in the 2027-2028 academic year. We have specific needs in Civil Procedure, Contracts, Legal Methods, and Torts, as well as Criminal Law, First Amendment, and Sales. We have flexibility in course needs to round out teaching packages. This position reports to the Dean of the Law School. WLC is a dynamic community of teachers and scholars. We pride ourselves on our dedication to our students, our engagement with teaching, and our scholarly impact. Many of our scholars are actively engaged in law reform efforts at both the state and federal level. The law school is committed to fostering an environment in which faculty, staff, and students from a variety of backgrounds, cultures, and personal experiences are welcomed and can thrive. Faculty and staff are active participants in our work to enhance inclusion and belonging. We welcome applications from members of historically underrepresented groups. Established in 1989, Widener Law Commonwealth is an independently accredited law school within Widener University. Located in Harrisburg, PA, the law school’s location in the capital of Pennsylvania provides impactful experiences for both our faculty and students. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES (including, but not limited to): Essential Duties: Teach first-year, upper division required, and elective classes both in the day and evening. We have specific needs in Civil Procedure, Contracts, Legal Methods, and Torts, as well as Criminal Law, First Amendment, and Sales. Engage in scholarship. Provide service to the Law School and the community. Secondary Responsibilities: Advise students and student organizations. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS (education/training and experience required): Required: J.D. degree from an ABA-accredited law school or an equivalent degree. Degree must be from an accredited University or College. A minimum of three years or related work or teaching experience. Strong academic or practice background in one or more of the courses to be taught. A capacity for and a commitment to be an effective teacher in the classroom. Demonstrated potential to be a productive scholar. A capacity for and commitment to work with and to mentor students. Demonstrated proficiency with Microsoft Office, including Word, Excel, PowerPoint, and Outlook. Demonstrated proficiency with Zoom. Preferred: Full-time or adjunct law school teaching experience. Experience teaching via distance education. Demonstrated proficiency with a learning management system, such as Canvas. PHYSICAL REQUIREMENTS AND/OR UNUSUAL HOURS: Must be available to teach day and evening division courses. All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Widener University is committed to fostering an inclusive community in which faculty, staff, and students from a variety of backgrounds, cultures, and personal experiences are welcomed and can thrive. We are an equal opportunity employer and are committed to providing equal educational and employment opportunities for all persons without regard to age, color, national origin, race, religion, disability, veteran status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, or status as a protected veteran. U.S. work authorization is required. Widener University, an independent, metropolitan, doctoral-intensive university, connects curricula to social issues through civic engagement. Dynamic teaching, active scholarship, personal attention and experiential learning are key components of the Widener Experience. Located in Chester, PA, Widener's main campus is nestled between Philadelphia, PA and Wilmington, DE, with Law Schools located in both Harrisburg and Wilmington. For more information about the university, please visit our website at www.widener.edu. EOE M/F/V/D Advertised: 23 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.widener.edu/en-us/job/493451/assistant-teaching-professor-mechanical-engineering": {
      "title": "Assistant Teaching Professor, Mechanical Engineering",
      "description": "Assistant Teaching Professor, Mechanical Engineering Apply now Job no: 493451 Work type: Faculty (Non-tenure track) Location: Chester Categories: Faculty The Department of Mechanical Engineering invites applications for an Assistant Teaching Professor position reporting to the Chair of Mechanical Engineering, this is a full-time, non-tenure track position beginning in Fall 2027. The successful candidate will primarily teach undergraduate and graduate courses in Mechanical Engineering, including foundational engineering courses. Additional teaching assignments may be based on the candidate's expertise and departmental needs. In addition to teaching, the successful candidate will advise and mentor undergraduate students, contribute to ABET assessment and accreditation activities, support continuous improvement initiatives, participate in student recruitment, retention, and outreach efforts, and engage in service to the department, the School of Engineering, and the University. The candidate is also expected to collaborate with faculty, staff, and students in advancing the educational mission and strategic goals of the Department of Mechanical Engineering and the School of Engineering. The review of applications will start on December 15 th , 2026. This is a full-time non-tenure track position subject to renewal as defined in the Widener University Faculty Handbook. To apply: Complete the online application form at widener.edu/jobs. Submit the following as a single PDF: A Cover Letter that addresses three items: Your preparation to teach engineering courses at Widener University. Your preparation to advise senior project students. A CV. A statement of teaching philosophy Evidence of teaching effectiveness, consisting at minimum of course evaluations, one sample syllabus, and one sample assignment. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES (including, but not limited to) : Essential duties: Teach undergraduate courses in Mechanical Engineering and related engineering disciplines such as Materials, Kinematics, Vibrations, or Design. Teach foundational engineering courses such as Statics, Dynamics, Mechanics of Deformable Bodies, and Computer-Aided Design (CAD), as assigned. Develop and deliver high-quality instruction using effective and inclusive teaching practices. Advise and mentor undergraduate students on academic and professional development. Supervise and mentor Mechanical Engineering Senior Design (Capstone) projects. Contribute to ABET assessment, accreditation, and continuous improvement activities, including course assessment and documentation. Participate in student recruitment, retention, outreach, and other initiatives that support enrollment growth and student success. Collaborate with faculty and staff to support the educational mission and strategic goals of the Department of Mechanical Engineering and the School of Engineering. Secondary Responsibilities: Participate in faculty governance and serve on departmental, school, and university committees, as appropriate. Attend and actively participate in department meetings, school meetings, commencement, open houses, and other academic and university events. Perform other duties consistent with the responsibilities of a visiting faculty appointment, as assigned by the Department Chair or Dean. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS (education/training and experience required) : Required: Ph.D. in Mechanical Engineering or a closely related field from an accredited institution. Degree must be from an accredited University or College. Demonstrated teaching ability in mechanical engineering courses. Ability to work collaboratively in a diverse academic environment. Preferred: Experience in advising students. Experience with industrial collaboration at university setting. PHYSICAL REQUIREMENTS AND/OR UNUSUAL HOURS: Occasional evening and weekend hours may be required to support events and organizational activities. Ability to remain seated and work at a computer for extended periods of time. Ability to communicate effectively by telephone and respond to inquiries in a timely and professional manner. Ability to move between offices, floors, and campus buildings. All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Widener University is committed to fostering an inclusive community in which faculty, staff, and students from a variety of backgrounds, cultures, and personal experiences are welcomed and can thrive. We are an equal opportunity employer and are committed to providing equal educational and employment opportunities for all persons without regard to age, color, national origin, race, religion, disability, veteran status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, or status as a protected veteran. U.S. work authorization is required. Some positions may be eligible for a hybrid or remote work arrangement that may include a partially or fully remote work location, consistent with Widener’s Flexible Work policy. Widener University, an independent, metropolitan, doctoral-intensive university, connects curricula to social issues through civic engagement. Dynamic teaching, active scholarship, personal attention and experiential learning are key components of the Widener Experience. Located in Chester, PA, Widener's main campus is nestled between Philadelphia, PA and Wilmington, DE, with Law Schools located in both Harrisburg and Wilmington. For more information about the university, please visit our website at www.widener.edu. EOE M/F/V/D Advertised: 22 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.widener.edu/en-us/job/493453/visiting-assistant-professor-of-psychology": {
      "title": "Visiting Assistant Professor of Psychology",
      "description": "Visiting Assistant Professor of Psychology Apply now Job no: 493453 Work type: Faculty (Non-tenure track) Location: Chester Categories: Faculty Reporting to the Dean of the College of Arts & Sciences, the Visiting Assistant Professor of Psychology is a full-time non-tenure-track position for the Spring 2027 semester. The undergraduate Psychology department at Widener University currently seeks applicants for a one-semester, full time visiting position for Spring 2027. This position will have a 4 course teaching load. The candidate is also expected to serve as an academic advisor to undergraduate psychology majors. The area of expertise is open, and applicants from all subfields of psychology will be considered. Successful candidates will join the undergraduate Psychology department at Widener University, which serves a large number of majors and also contributes to many of Widener’s pre-professional programs. Psychology faculty at Widener are dedicated to providing students with academic skills and experiential learning that help students find success in post-baccalaureate graduate programs and the workforce. Those candidates experienced in, or who would enjoy, working with a diverse range of faculty, staff and students, and those who can contribute to the climate of inclusivity are encouraged to apply. Applicants must submit a cover letter, curriculum vita, statement of teaching philosophy, and evidence of teaching effectiveness. Additionally, three confidential letters of reference must be sent to artsandsciences@widener.edu. Applications will be reviewed starting November 1, 2026. Only those selected for an interview will be contacted. For more information about Widener University and the Department of Psychology, see www.widener.edu . DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES (including, but not limited to) : Essential duties: Teaching Teach four courses, including Introduction to Psychology and an upper-level elective in the candidate’s area of expertise Develop course materials and assessments that foster critical thinking and content mastery. Evaluate student performance and provide constructive feedback. Maintain regular weekly office hours to provide academic support and guidance to students enrolled in assigned courses. Mentorship Advise and mentor undergraduate students related to course selection, majors, and career development. Service Attend division, College, and University faculty meetings and contribute to department initiatives. Secondary Responsibilities: Perform other job-related duties as assigned and participate in the intellectual life of the Department, the College of Arts & Sciences, and Widener University. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS (education/training and experience required) : Required: Ph.D. or advanced degree in psychology from an accredited institution. ABD candidates will be considered. Degree must be from an accredited University or College. Strong communication and interpersonal skills. Experience and commitment to excellence in teaching at the college level. Commitment to equity, inclusion and belonging in teaching and service. Preferred: Two or more years of teaching experience in Psychology Experience in advising undergraduate students. PHYSICAL REQUIREMENTS AND/OR UNUSUAL HOURS: Must be available for in-person instruction. Must be able to sit for extended periods while working at a computer or grading papers, and the ability lecture in a classroom. Must have the ability to speak clearly and audibly to deliver lectures, facilitate classroom discussions and interact with students and colleagues. Must have the ability to move around campus to attend meetings and teach in different classrooms. Minimal lifting may be involved for tasks such as carrying materials to class or moving books. All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Widener University is committed to fostering an inclusive community in which faculty, staff, and students from a variety of backgrounds, cultures, and personal experiences are welcomed and can thrive. We are an equal opportunity employer and are committed to providing equal educational and employment opportunities for all persons without regard to age, color, national origin, race, religion, disability, veteran status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, or status as a protected veteran. U.S. work authorization is required. Widener University, an independent, metropolitan, doctoral-intensive university, connects curricula to social issues through civic engagement. Dynamic teaching, active scholarship, personal attention and experiential learning are key components of the Widener Experience. Located in Chester, PA, Widener's main campus is nestled between Philadelphia, PA and Wilmington, DE, with Law Schools located in both Harrisburg and Wilmington. For more information about the university, please visit our website at www.widener.edu. EOE M/F/V/D Advertised: 22 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.widener.edu/en-us/job/493454/assistant-professor-of-psychology": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Psychology",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Psychology Apply now Job no: 493454 Work type: Faculty (Tenure track) Location: Chester Categories: Faculty Reporting to the Dean of the College of Arts & Sciences, the Assistant Professor of Psychology is a full-time tenure-track position beginning in Fall 2027. The undergraduate Psychology department at Widener University is currently seeking to fill two (2) Assistant Professor of Psychology – Tenure Track positions in the College of Arts and Sciences to start Fall 2027. Successful candidates will have demonstrated a strong commitment to excellence in undergraduate teaching and advising, will be ready to pursue an active research program that will include significant participation by undergraduate students, and will be expected to contribute to departmental, campus, and professional service. The field of expertise is open, and applicants from all subfields of psychology will be considered. Successful candidates will join the undergraduate Psychology department at Widener University, which serves a large number of majors and also contributes to many of Widener’s pre-professional programs. Psychology faculty at Widener are dedicated to providing students with academic skills and experiential learning that help students find success in post-baccalaureate graduate programs and the workforce. Faculty are expected to teach 21 credit hours per year, often through a 4/3 Fall/Spring course load. Widener University is committed to fostering an environment in which faculty, staff, and students from a variety of backgrounds, cultures, and personal experiences are welcomed and thrive. The College of Arts and Sciences puts high priority on inclusive pedagogy, mentoring, and community engagement. Those candidates experienced in, or who would enjoy, working with a diverse range of faculty, staff, and students are encouraged to apply. Applicants must submit a cover letter, curriculum vita, statement of teaching philosophy, statement of research, and evidence of teaching effectiveness. Additionally, three confidential letters of reference must be sent to artsandsciences@widener.edu. Applications will be reviewed starting November 1 st and will continue until January 2027. Interviews will be conducted in the Spring. Only those selected for an interview will be contacted. For more information about Widener University and the Department of Psychology, see www.widener.edu . DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES (including, but not limited to) : Essential duties: Teaching Teach undergraduate level courses in the Psychology department’s core curriculum (introductory psychology, statistics with lab, research methodology, capstone courses). Develop and teach upper-level psychology courses in the faculty member’s content area. Maintain regular weekly office hours to provide academic support and guidance to students enrolled in assigned courses. Mentorship Advise undergraduate students related to course selection, majors, and career development. Scholarship and Academic Citizenship Engage in research conducted with undergraduate students, publication of research activities, and academic citizenship at all levels within the university. Attend division, College, and University faculty meetings and contribute to academic citizenship initiatives. Secondary Responsibilities: Perform other job-related duties as assigned and participation in the intellectual life of the Department, the College of Arts & Sciences, and the University. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS (education/training and experience required) : Required: Ph.D. in psychology from an accredited institution. ABD candidates may be considered if the Ph.D. will be completed by May 2027. Evidence for a commitment to undergraduate education. Clear evidence of scholarly potential. Strong communication and interpersonal skills. Experience and commitment to excellence in teaching at the college level. Commitment to equity, inclusion, and belonging in teaching and service. Preferred: Two or more years of teaching experience in Psychology. Experience in teaching courses with a focus on statistics, research methodology, and/or experiential learning. While all areas of specialty will be considered, preference will be given to faculty who can expand the department’s offerings in cognitive psychology, clinical/counseling psychology, neuroscience, health psychology, forensic psychology, and community psychology. PHYSICAL REQUIREMENTS AND/OR UNUSUAL HOURS: Must be available for in-person instruction. Must be able to sit for extended periods while working at a computer or grading papers, and the ability lecture in a classroom. Must have the ability to speak clearly and audibly to deliver lectures, facilitate classroom discussions and interact with students and colleagues. Must have the ability to move around campus to attend meetings and teach in different classrooms. Minimal lifting may be involved for tasks such as carrying materials to class or moving books. All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Widener University is committed to fostering an inclusive community in which faculty, staff, and students from a variety of backgrounds, cultures, and personal experiences are welcomed and can thrive. We are an equal opportunity employer and are committed to providing equal educational and employment opportunities for all persons without regard to age, color, national origin, race, religion, disability, veteran status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, or status as a protected veteran. U.S. work authorization is required. Widener University, an independent, metropolitan, doctoral-intensive university, connects curricula to social issues through civic engagement. Dynamic teaching, active scholarship, personal attention and experiential learning are key components of the Widener Experience. Located in Chester, PA, Widener's main campus is nestled between Philadelphia, PA and Wilmington, DE, with Law Schools located in both Harrisburg and Wilmington. For more information about the university, please visit our website at www.widener.edu. EOE M/F/V/D Advertised: 22 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
